age_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#what-is-pinned-and-how","content":"No"},"115":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-51","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#what-is-pinned-and-how","content":"Version only, in `versions.json`"},"116":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-52","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#the-order-of-checks","content":"For the archive-based add-ons, the code follows the same order. Semantic Views is the clearest example:"},"117":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-53","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#the-order-of-checks","content":"`_expected_digest()` takes the digest from the kit's own pin, when the version matches. Otherwise it reads the release's `.sha256` file."},"118":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-54","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#the-order-of-checks","content":"`_download()` streams the archive to disk and computes its SHA-256 as it writes. It compares the digest with the expected one. On a mismatch it raises `Checksum mismatch` and deletes the archive."},"119":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-55","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#the-order-of-checks","content":"Only then does `_unpack()` run. It refuses any archive entry whose resolved path is outside the destination folder (`the Semantic Views archive holds a path outside its folder`). It also requires `tools/install.py` and at least one `build/install/*.sql` file."},"120":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-56","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#the-order-of-checks","content":"The UDF containers follow the same pattern. `fetch()` in `marketplace/udf_container.py` downloads to a temporary `.part` file, and `verify()` checks the size and the SHA-256 before the file is moved into the cache. A cached file is verified again when it is used, and removed if it fails. The mirror setting, `EXAKIT_UDF_CONTAINERS_MIRROR`, changes where the file comes from, not whether it is checked."},"121":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-57","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#the-order-of-checks","content":"Downloads must use `https://`. `_https()` in `marketplace/addons/semantic_views.py` refuses any other scheme and refuses a redirect that leaves HTTPS. The UDF path checks the redirect the same way."},"122":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-58","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#where-a-digest-comes-from-when-the-pin-does-not-match","content":"A pin is used only when the requested version is the pinned one. For other versions, the code can take the digest from the release itself:"},"123":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-59","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#where-a-digest-comes-from-when-the-pin-does-not-match","content":"Semantic Views reads the release's own `.sha256` file."},"124":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-60","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#where-a-digest-comes-from-when-the-pin-does-not-match","content":"Exasol Scheduler's `_digest()` reads the release's `.sha256` file, and then the release API."},"125":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-61","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#where-a-digest-comes-from-when-the-pin-does-not-match","content":"exapump's `exapump_expected_sha256()` falls back to a digest in the release API."},"126":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-62","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#where-a-digest-comes-from-when-the-pin-does-not-match","content":"These digests come from the same host as the file. They confirm that the download matches what that release says. They do not confirm that the release is one the kit's maintainers reviewed. Only the `versions.json` pin does that."},"127":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-63","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Three add-ons can install without a checksum when you set an environment variable to `1`. Without the variable, a missing checksum is refused."},"128":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-64","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Add-on"},"129":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-65","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Variable"},"130":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-66","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"What it does"},"131":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-67","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Where"},"132":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-68","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Exasol for VS Code"},"133":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-69","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"`EXAKIT_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_EXASOL_VSCODE`"},"134":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-70","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Installs the VSIX with no digest"},"135":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-71","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"`marketplace/addons/exasol_vscode.py`"},"136":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-72","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Exasol Scheduler"},"137":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-73","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"`EXAKIT_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_EXASOL_SCHEDULER`"},"138":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-74","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Installs the archive with no digest"},"139":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-75","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"`_download()` in `marketplace/addons/exasol_scheduler.py`"},"140":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-76","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"JSON Tables"},"141":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-77","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"`EXAKIT_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_JSON_TABLES`"},"142":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-78","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Installs the asset with no digest. The error message says \"a maintainer may explicitly set\" it"},"143":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-79","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"`_asset()` in `marketplace/addons/json_tables.py`"},"144":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-80","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#escape-hatches","content":"Do not set these for a normal install. A setting left in your shell applies to every later install."},"145":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-81","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#skills-are-copied-byte-for-byte","content":"The AI skills the kit ships from other repositories are copies of upstream files. The list is in `skills/upstream.json`, with the repository, a pinned commit or tag, and the path. `tools/sync_upstream_skills.py` writes `skills/upstream.lock.json`, which records a commit and a SHA-256 for every file, and each copy's license file. The script's `--check` mode fails when a copy differs from the lock, or when a pin falls behind the version the kit installs. The repository's notes say the copies are never edited by hand, and `tests/skills.sh` checks them against the lock."},"146":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-82","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#add-ons-the-kit-did-not-install","content":"The kit leaves an installation it did not make alone:"},"147":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-83","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#add-ons-the-kit-did-not-install","content":"`system_present()` in `marketplace/addons/dash_server.py` treats a `dash-server` command on your PATH as managed outside the kit when the kit has no receipt for it. The kit never installs over it, updates it or removes it."},"148":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-84","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#add-ons-the-kit-did-not-install","content":"`marketplace/addons/exasol_vscode.py` says: \"A user-installed extension remains the user's installation.\" It is listed as managed outside the kit and never offered again."},"149":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-85","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#removing-an-add-on","content":"`setup/help/exakit.json` says `--dry-run` \"previews the full plan and changes nothing\". The full uninstall keeps uv and the PATH line in your shell profile, because uv may be used by other tools and the profile line is shared (the comment above `exakit_uninstall_run()` in `setup/lib/common.sh`)."},"150":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-86","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#logs","content":"Each add-on's output goes to `logs/marketplace.log` in the kit home, created at mode 600. `run_logged()` in `marketplace/runtime.py` replaces the values of any environment variable whose name ends in `PASSWORD`, `TOKEN`, `_KEY` or `SECRET` before the output is written."},"151":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-87","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#limits","content":"Dash Server, dbt-exasol, pyexasol and the notebook package are pinned by version only. The kit checks no digest for them."},"152":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-88","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#limits","content":"Three add-ons have an escape hatch that installs without a digest."},"153":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-89","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#limits","content":"Digests taken from a release, rather than from `versions.json`, check the download against that release only."},"154":{"id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads-90","page_id":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/add-ons-and-downloads#limits","content":"The checks are SHA-256 digests. The add-on modules in `marketplace/` contain no signature verification."},"155":{"id":"/security/ai-clients","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"page","content":"AI clients and the read-only user","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients"},"156":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-0","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients","content":"The database user that AI clients connect as, what it may and may not do, and how a notebook question is approved before its SQL runs."},"157":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-1","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#who-connects-as-whom","content":"Who connects as whom"},"158":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-2","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"What the read-only user may do"},"159":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-3","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"What the server allows"},"160":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-4","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#the-server-has-no-token","content":"The server has no token"},"161":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-5","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"How a notebook question is approved"},"162":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-6","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-admin-can-do-from-the-terminal","content":"What the admin can do from the terminal"},"163":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-7","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/ai-clients#limits","content":"Limits"},"164":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-8","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients","content":"AI clients never connect as the admin. The kit creates a second database user, `mcp_readonly` (the default of `EXAKIT_MCP_READONLY_USER`, set in `setup/lib/common.sh`), and the Exasol MCP server connects as that user. The server runs beside the database, on 127.0.0.1, by default on port 4896 (`EXAKIT_MCP_SIDECAR_PORT`)."},"165":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-9","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#who-connects-as-whom","content":"The launcher starts the Exasol MCP server as a sidecar. Its own default connects it as the admin. The kit's comment in `setup/lib/mcp.sh` says so: \"the launcher connects it as SYS ... the kit connects it as its read-only MCP user.\" `_mcp_sidecar_definition()` in the same file writes the kit's definition, with `EXA_USER` set to the read-only user and `EXA_PASSWORD` read from `mcp_readonly_password`."},"166":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-10","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"`exakit_configure_mcp_readonly_access()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` grants exactly three system privileges and then checks that nothing else is held:"},"167":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-11","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"`CREATE SESSION`, so it can connect."},"168":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-12","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"`USE ANY SCHEMA`, so it can see every schema."},"169":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-13","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"`SELECT ANY TABLE`, so it can read every table and view, including ones created later."},"170":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-14","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"The grant comment says `SELECT ANY DICTIONARY` is deliberately not granted, so system dictionaries stay private. `_exakit_assert_mcp_readonly_posture()` checks that the user holds those three and no other system privilege, directly, through a role, or as an object grant. It also runs a write probe that must be rejected. The install stops if any check fails."},"171":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-15","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-read-only-user-may-do","content":"The read-only user's default schema is `STARTER_KIT` (`EXAKIT_MCP_READONLY_SCHEMAS`). It is only the landing schema for local uploads. It does not limit what the user may read."},"172":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-16","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`_mcp_sidecar_definition()` in `setup/lib/mcp.sh` sets these server settings:"},"173":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-17","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"Setting"},"174":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-18","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"Value"},"175":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-19","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`enable_read_query`"},"176":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-20","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`true`"},"177":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-21","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`enable_write_query`"},"178":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-22","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`false`"},"179":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-23","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`enable_preprocessor_tools`"},"180":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-24","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`false`"},"181":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-25","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`enable_read_bucketfs`"},"182":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-26","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`false`"},"183":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-27","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`enable_write_bucketfs`"},"184":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-28","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`false`"},"185":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-29","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`default_row_limit`"},"186":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-30","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`1000`"},"187":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-31","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"These are the server's own switches. The database's grants are the real limit. A client that sends a write is refused by the database, because the user has no write privilege."},"188":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-32","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-server-allows","content":"`SELECT ANY TABLE` means an AI client can read every column of every table, including columns that hold personal data. The sensitive-column rule in What is sent to an AI model limits what the kit's helpers send to a model. It does not limit what the MCP user can read."},"189":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-33","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#the-server-has-no-token","content":"The sidecar runs with `--no-auth` on 127.0.0.1 (`args` in `_mcp_sidecar_definition()`). The kit also sets `FASTMCP_HTTP_HOST_ORIGIN_PROTECTION` to `true`. This repository does not document what that setting blocks beyond its name. Any program on this computer that can reach port 4896 can call the server, and the server answers with whatever the read-only user may read."},"190":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-34","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"A notebook never runs SQL it has not shown you. The steps are in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/ask.py`:"},"191":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-35","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"`ask()` drafts one SQL statement from the approved column map. Nothing runs."},"192":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-36","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"You read the SQL. `Proposal.approve()` records its SHA-256 digest and writes an `ASK_APPROVE` line to the workflow log with the SQL text."},"193":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-37","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"`Proposal.edit()` replaces the SQL and clears the approval."},"194":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-38","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"`Proposal.run()` computes the digest of the text it is about to run. If it differs from the approved digest, it raises `This SQL has not been approved`."},"195":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-39","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"`_read_sql()` accepts text that starts with `SELECT` or `WITH` and has no semicolon before the end. This is a shape check. It is not a SQL parser. The database user and the server settings above are the real limit."},"196":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-40","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"`_mcp_settings()` refuses to run unless the connection user is not the admin, the password file is `mcp_readonly_password`, `connection.validated` is `true`, and the server URL is loopback `http`. The notebook sends no password. The sidecar holds the read-only login."},"197":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-41","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#how-a-notebook-question-is-approved","content":"The approval is per SQL text. It is not per session and not per question. The workflow log keeps the statement text, its digest and the row count."},"198":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-42","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#what-the-admin-can-do-from-the-terminal","content":"`exakit sql` runs as the admin. Its help entry in `setup/help/exakit.json` says so in its warning: \"This connects with the starter-kit profile, which is the ADMIN user and is NOT sandboxed. The read-only boundary is the MCP user, not this command.\" `exakit sql --write` runs writes as the admin. Use the read-only path for anything an AI client or an untrusted script should run."},"199":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-43","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#limits","content":"The MCP server has no authentication token. Its protection is the loopback address and the read-only user."},"200":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-44","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#limits","content":"The read-only user reads every table, so an AI client can query personal-data columns the kit would not send to a model."},"201":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-45","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#limits","content":"`_read_sql()` checks the shape of the text. It does not parse SQL. A statement that passes the shape check still runs as the read-only user, so the database decides what it may do."},"202":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-46","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#limits","content":"`exakit sql` is not read-only and not sandboxed."},"203":{"id":"/security/ai-clients-47","page_id":"/security/ai-clients","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/ai-clients#limits","content":"The read-only user's password is kept in the credentials folder (see Credentials)."},"204":{"id":"/security/credentials","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"page","content":"Credentials and passwords","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials"},"205":{"id":"/security/credentials-0","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials","content":"Where the kit keeps its passwords, who can read them, how they reach each tool, and what stays out of logs."},"206":{"id":"/security/credentials-1","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"Where they live"},"207":{"id":"/security/credentials-2","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials#file-permissions","content":"File permissions"},"208":{"id":"/security/credentials-3","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"How a password reaches a tool"},"209":{"id":"/security/credentials-4","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials#where-a-source-password-ends-up","content":"Where a source password ends up"},"210":{"id":"/security/credentials-5","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"What stays out of logs"},"211":{"id":"/security/credentials-6","page_id":"/security/credentials","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/credentials#limits","content":"Limits"},"212":{"id":"/security/credentials-7","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials","content":"The kit holds three kinds of secret: the database admin password (the SYS user), the password of the read-only user that AI clients use, and any model or Langfuse key you give the notebooks. Each secret is a file in one folder, `~/.exasol-starter-kit/credentials/`. A password is never written to `exakit.env`, and the manifest records the path of each credential file, not the password."},"213":{"id":"/security/credentials-8","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"File under `credentials/`"},"214":{"id":"/security/credentials-9","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"What it holds"},"215":{"id":"/security/credentials-10","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"Written by"},"216":{"id":"/security/credentials-11","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`runtime_sys_password`"},"217":{"id":"/security/credentials-12","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"The database admin (SYS) password. Its path is recorded as `runtime.password_file` in the kit's manifest"},"218":{"id":"/security/credentials-13","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`store_credential` in `setup/lib/common.sh`, called from `setup/lib/exapump.sh` and `exakit_configure_mcp_readonly_access`"},"219":{"id":"/security/credentials-14","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`mcp_readonly_password`"},"220":{"id":"/security/credentials-15","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"The password of the read-only MCP user"},"221":{"id":"/security/credentials-16","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`store_credential` in `exakit_configure_mcp_readonly_access`, `setup/lib/common.sh`"},"222":{"id":"/security/credentials-17","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`notebook_store_master_password`"},"223":{"id":"/security/credentials-18","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"The master password of the notebook connection store"},"224":{"id":"/security/credentials-19","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`_credential()` in `marketplace/addons/notebook.py`"},"225":{"id":"/security/credentials-20","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`notebook_model_api_key`, `notebook_langfuse_secret_key` and the other `notebook_*` files"},"226":{"id":"/security/credentials-21","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"A model or Langfuse key, only if you asked for `remember=True`"},"227":{"id":"/security/credentials-22","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`_write_private()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/setup_env.py`"},"228":{"id":"/security/credentials-23","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"Two more files hold a password in a different form:"},"229":{"id":"/security/credentials-24","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"`~/.exapump/config.toml` (the exapump profile) holds the admin password as a TOML value. `exapump_write_profile()` in `setup/lib/exapump.sh` writes it, then runs `chmod 600`."},"230":{"id":"/security/credentials-25","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-they-live","content":"The launcher's `sidecars.yaml` holds a reference to `mcp_readonly_password`, not the password. The file is written with mode 0600. `_mcp_sidecar_definition()` in `setup/lib/mcp.sh` says: \"The password is a file reference the editor resolves; it is never in this text.\""},"231":{"id":"/security/credentials-26","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#file-permissions","content":"The credentials folder is set to `700` by `store_credential()`. Each file is written as `<name>.tmp`, set to `600`, then renamed into place. An interrupted run cannot leave a truncated secret."},"232":{"id":"/security/credentials-27","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#file-permissions","content":"`_exakit_warn_unprotected_credentials()` checks that `600` took effect. On a filesystem that ignores Unix modes, such as a Windows drive mounted into WSL, the kit prints a warning once per run."},"233":{"id":"/security/credentials-28","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#file-permissions","content":"On Windows, `Protect-ExakitFile()` in `setup/lib/exakit-common.ps1` removes inherited permissions and grants full control to the current user only."},"234":{"id":"/security/credentials-29","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#file-permissions","content":"Generated passwords are 24 characters of `A-Z`, `a-z` and `0-9`. `generate_password()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` reads `/dev/urandom`. `New-ExakitPassword()` in `setup/lib/exakit-common.ps1` uses `RandomNumberGenerator`."},"235":{"id":"/security/credentials-30","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"Tool"},"236":{"id":"/security/credentials-31","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"How the password travels"},"237":{"id":"/security/credentials-32","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"Where to check it"},"238":{"id":"/security/credentials-33","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"exapump, for the profile"},"239":{"id":"/security/credentials-34","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The password goes in the environment variable `EXAKIT_PROFILE_PASSWORD`, read by a Python writer. It is never in argv"},"240":{"id":"/security/credentials-35","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"`exapump_write_profile()`, `setup/lib/exapump.sh`"},"241":{"id":"/security/credentials-36","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"exapump, for SQL that contains a password (`CREATE USER ... IDENTIFIED BY`)"},"242":{"id":"/security/credentials-37","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The SQL goes to exapump on standard input"},"243":{"id":"/security/credentials-38","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"`_exakit_run_exapump_sql()`, `setup/lib/common.sh`"},"244":{"id":"/security/credentials-39","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The read-only user's temporary config"},"245":{"id":"/security/credentials-40","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The admin and read-only passwords go in `EXAKIT_ADMIN_PASSWORD` and `EXAKIT_READONLY_PASSWORD`. The temporary file comes from `mktemp` and is removed on exit or failure"},"246":{"id":"/security/credentials-41","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"`_exakit_write_exapump_config()` and `exakit_track_sensitive_tmp()`, `setup/lib/common.sh`"},"247":{"id":"/security/credentials-42","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The Exasol MCP server"},"248":{"id":"/security/credentials-43","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The definition names the file, `EXA_PASSWORD` with a `valueFile`. The launcher reads the file"},"249":{"id":"/security/credentials-44","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"`_mcp_sidecar_definition()`, `setup/lib/mcp.sh`"},"250":{"id":"/security/credentials-45","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The notebook connection store"},"251":{"id":"/security/credentials-46","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"The master password is read from its file and passed as `SCS_MASTER_PASSWORD` to the store tool"},"252":{"id":"/security/credentials-47","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"`marketplace/addons/notebook.py`, around the `SCS_MASTER_PASSWORD` line"},"253":{"id":"/security/credentials-48","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"A source's password (virtual schemas)"},"254":{"id":"/security/credentials-49","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"You type or paste it. It goes to the engine on standard input. A key from a file goes to `paste.secret` at mode 600, which is deleted after use"},"255":{"id":"/security/credentials-50","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"`_exakit_vs_tui_paste()`, `setup/lib/virtual-schemas.sh`"},"256":{"id":"/security/credentials-51","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#how-a-password-reaches-a-tool","content":"A child process's environment is readable by its owner and by root, not by other users. The kit's comments rely on that. Each variable is unset again after the call that needs it."},"257":{"id":"/security/credentials-52","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#where-a-source-password-ends-up","content":"For a virtual schema the engine runs `CREATE CONNECTION ... TO <url> USER <user> IDENTIFIED BY <secret>` (`create_connection()` in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/lifecycle.py`). The password is therefore stored in the database, in the CONNECTION object. The kit does not check how the database stores that object. See the CREATE CONNECTION reference for the syntax."},"258":{"id":"/security/credentials-53","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"The installer log is created at mode 600 (`setup/lib/common.sh`, the `EXAKIT_LOG_FILE` block)."},"259":{"id":"/security/credentials-54","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"`_exakit_redact_mcp_secret_output()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` replaces the password, and the value after `IDENTIFIED BY`, before any `CREATE USER` or `ALTER USER` output is logged or shown."},"260":{"id":"/security/credentials-55","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"`run_logged()` in `marketplace/runtime.py` replaces the value of every child environment variable whose name ends in `PASSWORD`, `TOKEN`, `_KEY` or `SECRET` with `[redacted]`. The marketplace log is set to mode 600."},"261":{"id":"/security/credentials-56","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"`setup/lib/envfile.sh` refuses a settings-file name that contains `PASSWORD`, `API_KEY`, `TOKEN` or `SECRET`. A secret cannot come from `exakit.env`."},"262":{"id":"/security/credentials-57","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"`ModelConfig` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/model_config.py` declares the key with `field(repr=False)`. `redact_secrets()` removes the key and custom header values in plain, URL-encoded and base64 forms, and whatever follows `Authorization`, `Bearer`, `Basic` or an `api-key` label."},"263":{"id":"/security/credentials-58","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"`notebook/package/exakit_nb/workflow.py` refuses a log payload whose key is named like a secret, or whose DSN contains a password. A statement that mentions `IDENTIFIED BY`, `PASSWORD` or `API_KEY` is logged as `<redacted: credential-like SQL>`."},"264":{"id":"/security/credentials-59","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#what-stays-out-of-logs","content":"`setup_env.use_model()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/setup_env.py` asks for a key with a hidden prompt. The key stays in the kernel's environment unless you pass `remember=True`. `forget_model()` deletes the saved copy."},"265":{"id":"/security/credentials-60","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#limits","content":"`exakit sql` connects with the admin profile. Anyone who can read your home folder as your user can read the admin password in `~/.exapump/config.toml`. So can root."},"266":{"id":"/security/credentials-61","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#limits","content":"The notebook connection store is encrypted, but its master password is a file in the same credentials folder. The store protects the connection details from a casual reader. It does not protect them from anyone who can read that folder."},"267":{"id":"/security/credentials-62","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#limits","content":"The kit has no scheduled password rotation. When `mcp-setup` runs, it reapplies the stored read-only password with `ALTER USER`. It generates a new one only when the stored value is missing or fails validation."},"268":{"id":"/security/credentials-63","page_id":"/security/credentials","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/credentials#limits","content":"The protection of the admin password is file permissions. No code in the kit calls the operating system's keychain."},"269":{"id":"/security","page_id":"/security","type":"page","content":"Security","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security"},"270":{"id":"/security-0","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security","content":"How the kit protects your data, credentials and computer, with the file and function behind each claim."},"271":{"id":"/security-1","page_id":"/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security#the-trust-boundaries","content":"The trust boundaries"},"272":{"id":"/security-2","page_id":"/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security#pages","content":"Pages"},"273":{"id":"/security-3","page_id":"/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Known limits, from the code"},"274":{"id":"/security-4","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security","content":"This section is for a security reviewer, a platform engineer or a DBA. Every statement names the file and the function that does it, and quotes the behaviour from the code. Where the code does not do something, the page says so. Most pages end with a list of their limits."},"275":{"id":"/security-5","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security","content":"The kit runs on your computer. It installs a local Exasol database, connects AI clients to it, and gives notebooks a way to ask questions. Its outbound calls are listed on the Network and TLS page."},"276":{"id":"/security-6","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#the-trust-boundaries","content":"Two charts show where data and credentials move. The first is inside this computer."},"277":{"id":"/security-7","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#the-trust-boundaries","content":"The second is what leaves this computer, and when."},"278":{"id":"/security-8","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"These are the places where the code does less than a reader might assume. Each is explained on its page."},"279":{"id":"/security-9","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Limit"},"280":{"id":"/security-10","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"What the code does"},"281":{"id":"/security-11","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Where"},"282":{"id":"/security-12","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"`exakit sql` runs as the admin user"},"283":{"id":"/security-13","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"It uses the exapump admin profile and is not sandboxed. The read-only boundary is the MCP user, not this command"},"284":{"id":"/security-14","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"AI clients"},"285":{"id":"/security-15","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"The read-only user reads every table"},"286":{"id":"/security-16","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"It holds `SELECT ANY TABLE`, so an AI client can query personal-data columns. The sensitive-column rule limits what the kit sends to a model, not what the MCP user can read"},"287":{"id":"/security-17","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"AI clients"},"288":{"id":"/security-18","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"The MCP server has no token"},"289":{"id":"/security-19","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"It runs with `--no-auth` on 127.0.0.1. Any program on this computer that reaches the port can send read queries"},"290":{"id":"/security-20","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"AI clients"},"291":{"id":"/security-21","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"The MCP server connection is not pinned here"},"292":{"id":"/security-22","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"The sidecar definition sets `EXA_DSN` to the plain host and port and `EXA_SSL_CERT_VALIDATION` to `false`. The certificate pin is added to the other clients only"},"293":{"id":"/security-23","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Network and TLS"},"294":{"id":"/security-24","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"One helper sends row text"},"295":{"id":"/security-25","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"`nb.themes()` sends the text of the rows you select to your model, up to 600 characters each. The sensitive-column rule does not apply to it"},"296":{"id":"/security-26","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"What is sent to a model"},"297":{"id":"/security-27","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Three add-ons can skip their checksum"},"298":{"id":"/security-28","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Setting `EXAKIT_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_EXASOL_VSCODE`, `EXAKIT_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_EXASOL_SCHEDULER` or `EXAKIT_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_JSON_TABLES` to `1` installs without a digest"},"299":{"id":"/security-29","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Add-ons and downloads"},"300":{"id":"/security-30","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"A pasted connection string can lower TLS"},"301":{"id":"/security-31","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"A SQL Server string with `encrypt=false` sets `tls=disable` for that source. You see the reading before you confirm it"},"302":{"id":"/security-32","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Network and TLS"},"303":{"id":"/security-33","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Your passwords are plain text in a 0600 file"},"304":{"id":"/security-34","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"The admin password sits in the exapump profile, and the notebook store master password sits beside its store. Both are protected by file permissions only"},"305":{"id":"/security-35","page_id":"/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security#known-limits-from-the-code","content":"Credentials"},"306":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"page","content":"Network and TLS","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls"},"307":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-0","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls","content":"The database certificate pin, the addresses the kit listens on, every outbound call it makes, and how external sources are verified."},"308":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-1","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"The database certificate is pinned"},"309":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-2","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#installs-made-before-the-pin-existed","content":"Installs made before the pin existed"},"310":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-3","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-mcp-server-connection-is-not-pinned-in-this-code","content":"The MCP server connection is not pinned in this code"},"311":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-4","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Addresses the kit listens on"},"312":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-5","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Every outbound call the kit makes"},"313":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-6","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#external-sources","content":"External sources"},"314":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-7","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/network-and-tls#limits","content":"Limits"},"315":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-8","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls","content":"The kit listens on this computer only. Its outbound calls are few and listed below. The one that needs the most care is the database connection, because the database uses a self-signed certificate that no client can check against a certificate authority."},"316":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-9","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"The kit records the database's certificate fingerprint once, then every client it builds checks that the port still serves that certificate."},"317":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-10","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"**What is recorded.** The SHA-256 of the server's DER certificate, as lowercase hex. `fingerprint()` in `setup/lib/tls_pin.py` computes it. It reads the certificate without validating it, because reading it is the point. The value is stored in the manifest as `runtime.tls_fingerprint`, with the time in `runtime.tls_fingerprint_at`."},"318":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-11","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"**When it is recorded.** When the kit deploys or starts its own database, `exakit_tls_record()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` records the certificate. This is trust on first use, as the code's comment calls it."},"319":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-12","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"**Who checks it.** The exapump profile gets `certificate_fingerprint` (`exapump_write_profile()` in `setup/lib/exapump.sh`). A pyexasol connection gets the DSN `host/<fingerprint>:port` (`exakit_tls_pinned_dsn()` in `setup/lib/common.sh`). The notebook's `connect()` pins the same way (`_pinned()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/__init__.py`)."},"320":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-13","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"**What a mismatch does.** A client refuses a certificate that does not match. `exakit tls-pin` compares the pinned and live values and changes nothing. `exakit tls-pin --accept` records the live certificate, after a question that defaults to no when a pin is being replaced. Its exit code is `6` when the live certificate is not the pinned one (`setup/help/exakit.json`)."},"321":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-14","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"The profiles also carry `validate_certificate = false`. That is deliberate: the certificate is self-signed, so the pin is the check. Without a recorded pin, nothing validates the certificate."},"322":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-15","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-database-certificate-is-pinned","content":"Exasol's own documentation describes the same approach for clients that cannot verify a certificate: a SHA-256 fingerprint, which changes whenever the certificate is replaced. See Upload TLS certificate."},"323":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-16","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#installs-made-before-the-pin-existed","content":"`exakit_tls_ensure_pinned()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` records a pin the first time a kit command finds the database running and answering. The pin then trusts whatever answered on the port at that time. The code does not compare it with anything earlier."},"324":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-17","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-mcp-server-connection-is-not-pinned-in-this-code","content":"`_mcp_sidecar_definition()` in `setup/lib/mcp.sh` sets the server's connection like this:"},"325":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-18","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#the-mcp-server-connection-is-not-pinned-in-this-code","content":"`exakit_tls_rewrite_clients()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` says that \"the launcher's sidecar entries hold no DSN\". The pin reaches the stdio form of the server's `EXA_DSN`, and the other kit clients. It does not reach the sidecar definition in this repository. Whether the launcher adds a pin elsewhere is outside this repository, so this page does not claim one."},"326":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-19","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Listener"},"327":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-20","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Address"},"328":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-21","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Source"},"329":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-22","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Notebook documentation server"},"330":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-23","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"`127.0.0.1` (the page is shown as `localhost`)"},"331":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-24","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"`BIND` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/docs.py`. Its comment says \"no other computer reaches it\""},"332":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-25","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Exasol MCP server"},"333":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-26","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"`127.0.0.1:4896`, with `--no-auth`"},"334":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-27","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"`hostIP` and `args` in `_mcp_sidecar_definition()`, `setup/lib/mcp.sh`"},"335":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-28","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"Database"},"336":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-29","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"The port recorded in the manifest, on this computer"},"337":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-30","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"`runtime.dsn` in the manifest"},"338":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-31","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#addresses-the-kit-listens-on","content":"The MCP server has no token. See AI clients."},"339":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-32","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Call"},"340":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-33","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Destination"},"341":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-34","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"When"},"342":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-35","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Source"},"343":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-36","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Install and update downloads"},"344":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-37","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"GitHub release URLs and the launcher's S3 bucket, over HTTPS"},"345":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-38","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"When you install, update or repair"},"346":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-39","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"`versions.json`; see Add-ons and downloads"},"347":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-40","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Public IP lookup"},"348":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-41","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"`https://checkip.amazonaws.com`, one request per run, 3-second limit"},"349":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-42","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"When a source command runs. Skipped with `EXAKIT_NO_IP_LOOKUP=1`"},"350":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-43","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"`setup/virtual-schemas/engine/publicip.py`"},"351":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-44","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Your model"},"352":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-45","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"The address in `EXAKIT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL`"},"353":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-46","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Only when you use a model helper"},"354":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-47","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"What is sent to a model"},"355":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-48","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Langfuse"},"356":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-49","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"The address in `LANGFUSE_BASE_URL`"},"357":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-50","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Only when tracing is on"},"358":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-51","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"What is sent to a model"},"359":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-52","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"External sources"},"360":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-53","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"The source you connected"},"361":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-54","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"When the database reads it"},"362":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-55","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"External sources below"},"363":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-56","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"The public IP is one address and a newline. The module says it is \"advice, never a step\". It is for allow-listing this computer at a source that filters by address. If the lookup fails, the address is reported as unknown."},"364":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-57","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#every-outbound-call-the-kit-makes","content":"Telemetry is turned off by default. `marketplace/kit_telemetry.py` sets `EXASOL_TELEMETRY_DISABLE`, `DBT_SEND_ANONYMOUS_USAGE_STATS=false` and `MLFLOW_DISABLE_TELEMETRY=true` for every process the kit starts, unless you set those variables yourself."},"365":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-58","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#external-sources","content":"A source is read where it lives. The database connects to it through a virtual schema, and the connection's TLS setting decides how the certificate is checked."},"366":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-59","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#external-sources","content":"**The default is `verify`.** `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/paste.py` says \"tls stays at verify (TCPS, the kit's default)\". The engine's failure advice in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/lifecycle.py`, `tls_fix()`, names the fix: \"fix the server certificate\", or give the CA that signs it with `--set ca=<PEM file or bundle>`."},"367":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-60","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#external-sources","content":"**`require` encrypts without checking.** `tls_fix()` says it is used \"only knowingly\". The engine's own advice in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/sql.py` calls it \"encrypts without checking the certificate\"."},"368":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-61","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#external-sources","content":"**A pasted string can change the setting.** `tls_sqlserver()` in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/paste.py` maps `encrypt=false` to `disable`, and `TrustServerCertificate=true` to `require`. Those values come from the string you paste, not from the kit's defaults. Before you confirm the connection, the kit shows the reading back with its secrets masked. The shell prints it as `Read: ...` in `_exakit_vs_tui_paste()` in `setup/lib/virtual-schemas.sh`."},"369":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-62","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#external-sources","content":"**The kit does not lower the setting by itself.** When the paste does not say otherwise, the engine keeps `verify`. An unknown `encrypt` value is reported, and `tls` stays at its default."},"370":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-63","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#limits","content":"The pin is recorded on first use. It trusts whatever answered on the port at that moment."},"371":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-64","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#limits","content":"The MCP server's connection to the database is not pinned in this repository."},"372":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-65","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#limits","content":"The database connection uses `validate_certificate = false` alongside the pin."},"373":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-66","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#limits","content":"A pasted SQL Server string can set `tls=disable` for a source. The kit shows that reading before you confirm, but the choice is yours."},"374":{"id":"/security/network-and-tls-67","page_id":"/security/network-and-tls","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/network-and-tls#limits","content":"Telemetry is off only for the processes the kit starts. Tools you run yourself follow their own settings."},"375":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","type":"page","content":"Report a security issue","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security/report-an-issue"},"376":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-0","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue","content":"Where to send a vulnerability, what to include, and what the repository does and does not publish about security reports."},"377":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-1","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-the-repository-does-not-publish","content":"What the repository does not publish"},"378":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-2","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"What to include"},"379":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-3","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-not-to-send","content":"What not to send"},"380":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-4","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/report-an-issue#a-problem-that-is-not-a-vulnerability","content":"A problem that is not a vulnerability"},"381":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-5","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue","content":"Send a suspected vulnerability to **plg.connect\\@exasol.com**. That is the address the documentation's Feedback button uses (`FEEDBACK_ADDRESS` in `notebook/docs/components/feedback.tsx`), and the one the repository's issue chooser offers as \"Write to the team\" (`.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/config.yml`)."},"382":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-6","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-the-repository-does-not-publish","content":"There is no `SECURITY.md` file in the repository root. This page does not link one, and it does not invent a disclosure process, a response time or a security contact other than the address above."},"383":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-7","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-the-repository-does-not-publish","content":"The repository does not state how the team handles a report after it arrives. Nothing in this section describes that process."},"384":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-8","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"Describe what you did and what you saw. These help the team reproduce it:"},"385":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-9","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"The kit version, from `exakit status --json`."},"386":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-10","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"The command or notebook cell, and the file or page it came from."},"387":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-11","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"Your operating system and whether you ran the Windows or the Unix installer."},"388":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-12","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"What you expected, and what happened instead."},"389":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-13","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-to-include","content":"Read the output before you send it. The kit redacts the secrets listed on the Credentials page in the places that page names. Logs can still hold your data, file paths and table names."},"390":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-14","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#what-not-to-send","content":"Do not send the contents of `~/.exasol-starter-kit/credentials/`, `~/.exapump/config.toml`, the notebook connection store, or an `exakit.env` file. Describe the problem without them."},"391":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-15","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#a-problem-that-is-not-a-vulnerability","content":"For an ordinary problem with the kit, the repository's issue chooser suggests an issue with the output of `exakit status --json` and `exakit logs`. Check that output for anything private before you post it in public."},"392":{"id":"/security/report-an-issue-16","page_id":"/security/report-an-issue","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/report-an-issue#a-problem-that-is-not-a-vulnerability","content":"`exakit tls-pin` with no options compares the pinned certificate with the live one and changes nothing. `exakit mcp-doctor` tests the AI connection and repairs it, so run it only when you are ready for a repair."},"393":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"page","content":"What is sent to an AI model","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model"},"394":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-0","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","content":"Which helper sends what to your model, how sensitive columns are found and kept out, where a key may travel, and when tracing is on."},"395":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-1","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Which helper sends what"},"396":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-2","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#a-flag-column-gives-its-values","content":"A flag column gives its values"},"397":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-3","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"How sensitive columns are found"},"398":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-4","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"Where a key may travel"},"399":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-5","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#tracing-is-opt-in","content":"Tracing is opt-in"},"400":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-6","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#limits","content":"Limits"},"401":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-7","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","content":"The kit calls a model only when you set one. Set `EXAKIT_MODEL_ID` and `EXAKIT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL`, and the helpers below call that address. The request is Chat Completions. Every model client is built by `create_client()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/model_config.py`. The model is your choice, and the kit does not check the provider's data-retention terms."},"402":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-8","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Helper"},"403":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-9","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Where it is"},"404":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-10","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"What the model receives"},"405":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-11","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"What it never receives"},"406":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-12","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`ask()`"},"407":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-13","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`notebook/package/exakit_nb/ask.py`, `described()`"},"408":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-14","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"The table name, the descriptor version, the slots, and each column's role, evidence and note. The question. A sensitive column appears by name and role only, with the instruction \"never select, group or filter its values\""},"409":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-15","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Rows. Values of sensitive columns"},"410":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-16","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Model notes"},"411":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-17","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`setup/lib/describe_service.py`, `model_notes()`"},"412":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-18","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"For each non-sensitive column: its name, type, role, evidence and up to five of its most frequent values"},"413":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-19","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Sensitive columns, which are skipped entirely"},"414":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-20","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Semantic Views drafting"},"415":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-21","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`setup/lib/describe_service.py`, `semantic_ai()`, with `ai_facts()` in `setup/lib/descriptor/semantic_model.py`"},"416":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-22","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Entity names and tables, grains, relationships, dimension and fact names and comments, metric expressions, and up to ten values of a category dimension"},"417":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-23","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Private facts. Sensitive columns carry no values"},"418":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-24","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Feature ideas with `ai=True`"},"419":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-25","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`notebook/package/exakit_nb/lab.py`, `feature_ideas()`"},"420":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-26","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Non-sensitive column names and roles, the target name, and model scores"},"421":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-27","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Row values"},"422":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-28","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`nb.themes()`"},"423":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-29","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`notebook/package/exakit_nb/themes.py`"},"424":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-30","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"The text of the rows in the sample you pass in, up to 600 characters each, one call per side of the label"},"425":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-31","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"Nothing is filtered. See the warning below"},"426":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-32","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#which-helper-sends-what","content":"`nb.themes()` sends row text to your model. It is the only helper that sends the content of rows. The kit does not check whether that text column is sensitive. The SQL in the notebook cell chooses the rows, so the sensitive-column rule does not apply to them."},"427":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-33","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#a-flag-column-gives-its-values","content":"A flag is a column with two values. For a flag, the evidence in the descriptor holds both values, and the evidence is sent to the model. `ask()`'s rules say so: \"A flag's evidence gives its values.\" So a non-sensitive flag such as `CANCELLED` reaches the model with its two values, as do the values of a category."},"428":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-34","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"`setup/lib/descriptor/roles.py` decides sensitivity before the role, and keeps it whatever the role is. Its docstring gives the rules. A text column is sensitive when:"},"429":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-35","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"90% or more of its values are e-mail addresses, phone numbers, IBANs or 5-digit postcodes, or"},"430":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-36","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"its name says postcode (ZIP, POSTAL, PLZ) and 90% of its values are shaped like one, or"},"431":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-37","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"its name says a person's name (`FIRST_NAME`, `SURNAME`, `CUSTOMER_NAME`, `DRIVER_NAME`), or"},"432":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-38","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"it is an address whose values are long and mostly distinct (`STREET_ADDRESS`, though not `ADDR_STATE`)."},"433":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-39","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"A sensitive column gets the role `identifier`. `_finish()` in `setup/lib/descriptor/profile.py` keeps counts for it and drops its top values, minimum and maximum. The module docstring of `setup/lib/descriptor/store.py` says the profile file holds \"no value of a sensitive column, because these files end up in bug reports.\""},"434":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-40","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#how-sensitive-columns-are-found","content":"Detection is a set of patterns. It can miss personal data that does not match them, for example a free-text note that contains a name. Check a column map before you approve it, and mark a column yourself if it is wrong."},"435":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-41","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"`resolve()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/model_config.py` refuses to build a client with a key unless the address passes `clear_text_ok()`:"},"436":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-42","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"`https://` to any host, or"},"437":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-43","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"`http://` only to `localhost`, a `.local` or `.internal` name, or a loopback, private or link-local address."},"438":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-44","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"Anything else raises `... would send EXAKIT_PROVIDER_API_KEY in clear text`, before a request is made. `setup_env.use_model()` applies the same test before it asks for a key, and prints a note instead."},"439":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-45","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"Two gaps follow from the code, and they are stated here so they are not assumed away:"},"440":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-46","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"With no key set, `resolve()` accepts a plain `http://` address to any host. No key is sent, but the prompts and answers travel in clear text."},"441":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-47","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#where-a-key-may-travel","content":"The `clear_text_ok()` test applies to the Langfuse address only inside `use_model()`. A Langfuse address set in your shell or in `exakit.env` is not checked."},"442":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-48","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#tracing-is-opt-in","content":"`tracing()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/__init__.py` returns true only when `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY`, `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` and `LANGFUSE_BASE_URL` (or `LANGFUSE_HOST`) are all set. Without them, `create_client()` builds a plain OpenAI client, and nothing is sent to Langfuse. With them, `model_client()` returns Langfuse's wrapper for the same OpenAI call, and each call is recorded in your Langfuse project. The kit adds no filter to what the wrapper records beyond the rules in the table above."},"443":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-49","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#limits","content":"The kit sends what the table above lists. Your model provider handles that data under its own terms."},"444":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-50","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#limits","content":"A model that sees column names and values can infer what a sensitive column holds. The kit keeps the values out, not the inference."},"445":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-51","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#limits","content":"Plain `http://` to a public host, with no key, is not blocked."},"446":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-52","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#limits","content":"The Langfuse address rule is not applied outside `use_model()`."},"447":{"id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model-53","page_id":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-goes-to-a-model#limits","content":"`nb.themes()` sends the text of the rows you choose."},"448":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"page","content":"What the kit writes","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Security"],"tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes"},"449":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-0","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","content":"The schemas the kit creates, which of your tables it will change and when, the files it keeps on this computer, and what it leaves alone."},"450":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-1","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"Schemas in the database"},"451":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-2","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"Your tables"},"452":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-3","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#comments-the-kit-adds","content":"Comments the kit adds"},"453":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-4","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#files-on-this-computer","content":"Files on this computer"},"454":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-5","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-approved-column-maps","content":"The approved column maps"},"455":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-6","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-shell-profile","content":"The shell profile"},"456":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-7","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#what-the-kit-leaves-alone","content":"What the kit leaves alone"},"457":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-8","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#limits","content":"Limits"},"458":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-9","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","content":"The kit writes to two places: your database and your computer. This page lists what it creates, what it changes, and what it will not touch."},"459":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-10","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"Schema or object"},"460":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-11","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"What it holds"},"461":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-12","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"Created by"},"462":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-13","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`STARTER_KIT`"},"463":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-14","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"The default schema of the read-only user, where local uploads land. Created if it is missing"},"464":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-15","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`exakit_configure_mcp_readonly_access()`, `setup/lib/common.sh`"},"465":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-16","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"The sample dataset schemas, such as `AMAZON` and `TPCH`"},"466":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-17","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"The bundled sample data. Created by each dataset's own `01_create_schema.sql` in `data/datasets/<name>/`"},"467":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-18","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`exakit data-load <name>`"},"468":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-19","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`EXAKIT_PREPARED`"},"469":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-20","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"Joined views and typed, cleaned views you choose to create"},"470":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-21","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`SCHEMA = \"EXAKIT_PREPARED\"` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/prepare.py`, and `CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS EXAKIT_PREPARED` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/schema_map.py`"},"471":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-22","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`SEMANTIC_CATALOG`, `SEMANTIC_ADMIN`, `SEMANTIC_AGENT`"},"472":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-23","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"Exasol Semantic Views' own schemas. `SEMANTIC_CATALOG` holds the models' metadata, which any read-only client can read, for example `SEMANTIC_CATALOG.RELATIONSHIPS`"},"473":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-24","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`setup/lib/descriptor/schema.py` and `setup/lib/descriptor/publish.py`"},"474":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-25","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`SEM_<MODEL>`"},"475":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-26","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"A published semantic model's views"},"476":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-27","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`setup/lib/descriptor/publish.py`"},"477":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-28","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`VS_<NAME>`, and a CONNECTION named `EXAKIT_SRC_<NAME>`"},"478":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-29","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"An external source's virtual schema, and its connection"},"479":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-30","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#schemas-in-the-database","content":"`setup/virtual-schemas/engine/plan.py`, where `\"connection\": \"EXAKIT_SRC_%s\"` and `\"schema\": \"VS_%s\"` are set"},"480":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-31","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"The kit changes a table you own only when you tell it to. Three paths can do so."},"481":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-32","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"**Loading a file (`exakit data-load`, `nb.load_own_file()`).** The kit does not overwrite a table it did not load. `load_own_file()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/own_file.py` stops with \"already exists and was not loaded by load_own_file\" unless you pass `replace=True`. For `exakit data-load`, `EXAKIT_ON_EXISTING` decides:"},"482":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-33","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"`skip`, the default. A table with rows the kit did not load is left alone. The command exits with code 5 and changes nothing (`setup/lib/exapump.sh`)."},"483":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-34","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"`replace`. The table is dropped and rebuilt from the file. The code comment says \"DROP, not TRUNCATE\". The load receipt is removed with it."},"484":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-35","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"`append`. The file's rows are added on top of the existing rows."},"485":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-36","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"**Writing SQL from the terminal (`exakit sql --write`).** This runs as the admin, so it can change any table. See AI clients."},"486":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-37","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#your-tables","content":"**Approving a column map.** Approval writes a descriptor under `~/.exasol-starter-kit/descriptors/`. It changes the database only through Semantic Views, in the schemas listed above."},"487":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-38","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#comments-the-kit-adds","content":"When the kit loads a file, it marks the table with `COMMENT ON TABLE`. The mark holds the file's SHA-256, and it is written only on a table the kit has just created or replaced. That mark is how the kit recognises a table it loaded, so a later load can skip it. Outside that load, the kit issues no `ALTER TABLE` and no other `COMMENT ON`. A search of `setup/` and `notebook/package/exakit_nb/` confirms this. The `replace` and `append` paths above are the exception, because you chose them."},"488":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-39","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#comments-the-kit-adds","content":"`setup/lib/descriptor/schema.py` states the same rule for schema maps: \"the user's own schema and tables are never commented or altered.\""},"489":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-40","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#files-on-this-computer","content":"Everything is under the kit home, `~/.exasol-starter-kit` (or `EXAKIT_HOME`). Three rules apply to the files there:"},"490":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-41","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#files-on-this-computer","content":"**Secrets** are in `credentials/`. See Credentials."},"491":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-42","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#files-on-this-computer","content":"**Logs and records** are written at mode 600 on Unix. The workflow log is opened with `os.open(..., 0o600)` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/workflow.py`. The virtual schema log is \"created owner-only (mode 600)\" according to `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/vslog.py`."},"492":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-43","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#files-on-this-computer","content":"**Hand-off bundles** get mode 700 for folders and 600 for files, set by `handoff_bundle.py`. Those modes are not applied on Windows, which the code checks with `os.name != \"nt\"`."},"493":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-44","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-approved-column-maps","content":"Each approved column map is kept as a version, in `~/.exasol-starter-kit/descriptors/<SCHEMA>.<TABLE>/`. The module docstring of `setup/lib/descriptor/store.py` gives the layout:"},"494":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-45","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-approved-column-maps","content":"`v<n>.yaml` is written exclusively. \"A version file is created exclusively, so v(n) is never touched.\" An approval never rewrites an earlier version."},"495":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-46","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-approved-column-maps","content":"`current.yaml` is replaced through a temporary file and `os.replace`."},"496":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-47","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-approved-column-maps","content":"`draft.yaml` and `profile.json` hold the work in progress. The profile has no value of a sensitive column."},"497":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-48","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-approved-column-maps","content":"A whole schema's map (its tables and the links between them) is kept the same way, in `descriptors/<SCHEMA>/`, with `v<n>.json` files. `setup/lib/descriptor/schema.py` says those are never rewritten."},"498":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-49","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#the-shell-profile","content":"The installer adds the kit's `bin` folder to your shell profile, through `ensure_path_hint()` in `setup/lib/common.sh`. If `EXAKIT_NO_PROFILE_EDIT=1` is set, it prints the line for you to add instead. The full uninstall leaves that line in place, as the comment above `exakit_uninstall_run()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` says. Remove the line yourself if you want it gone."},"499":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-50","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#what-the-kit-leaves-alone","content":"A table holding rows the kit did not load, unless you choose `replace` or `append`."},"500":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-51","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#what-the-kit-leaves-alone","content":"A notebook you edited. The Notebooks page says an edited notebook is never overwritten."},"501":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-52","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#what-the-kit-leaves-alone","content":"Approved column maps, workflow logs and bundles. The Where things live page says an update leaves them alone."},"502":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-53","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#what-the-kit-leaves-alone","content":"An add-on installation the kit did not make. See Add-ons and downloads."},"503":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-54","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#limits","content":"`exakit data-load` with `replace` drops a table you own. On a terminal you are asked which policy applies. Without a terminal the policy is `skip`, unless `EXAKIT_ON_EXISTING` says otherwise."},"504":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-55","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#limits","content":"`exakit sql --write` can change any table, as the admin."},"505":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-56","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#limits","content":"On Windows, the Unix modes above are not applied. The NTFS permissions described in Credentials are the protection there."},"506":{"id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes-57","page_id":"/security/what-the-kit-writes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/security/what-the-kit-writes#limits","content":"The virtual schema log removes its files older than `KEEP_DAYS` when a new day's file is opened (`setup/virtual-schemas/engine/vslog.py`). This page found no pruning rule for the other logs."},"507":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","type":"page","content":"Build the dashboard query","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query"},"508":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","content":"How the notebook builds the dashboard's query from the approved roles, and how it names the dashboard."},"509":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#the-code","content":"The code"},"510":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#what-it-produces-for-amazon_sales","content":"What it produces for amazon_sales"},"511":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#before-anything-is-sent","content":"Before anything is sent"},"512":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#next","content":"Next"},"513":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","content":"A dashboard starts from one query. The notebook builds that query from the approved column map, so the group and the count follow the roles you approved and not a name you typed."},"514":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#the-code","content":"In `03_share`, the dataset's roles build the query. The group is the category, the count is named after the grain, and the title and name follow from both:"},"515":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#the-code","content":"`dataset.group_by()` returns the category. When the dataset has no category, it uses the entity, and then the target. The notebook prints a line when that happens."},"516":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#what-it-produces-for-amazon_sales","content":"The query: `SELECT CATEGORY AS CATEGORY, COUNT(*) AS ORDER_LINES FROM AMAZON.ORDER_LINES GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY ORDER_LINES DESC`"},"517":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#what-it-produces-for-amazon_sales","content":"The title: \"amazon_sales by category\""},"518":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#what-it-produces-for-amazon_sales","content":"The name: `amazon-sales-by-category`"},"519":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#what-it-produces-for-amazon_sales","content":"The count is called `ORDER_LINES` because the column map's grain says \"one row per order line\". The name comes from the grain, through `nb.count_name`."},"520":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#what-it-produces-for-amazon_sales","content":"`TITLE` and `APP_NAME` at the top of the notebook replace the generated title and name. The name identifies the dashboard on Dash Server."},"521":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#before-anything-is-sent","content":"The notebook prints the query and its digest first, so you can read the SQL before it goes anywhere. Nothing is sent to Dash Server until you run the next cell."},"522":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query#next","content":"Build the preview and check it on Preview, check, publish. Back to Communicate and deploy."},"523":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","type":"page","content":"Deploying a model","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model"},"524":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","content":"What the kit does and does not deploy from the Data Scientist notebooks, and where the in-database scoring path lives."},"525":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"Deploying a model"},"526":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"The kit does not deploy a model from these notebooks."},"527":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"The lab's fitted model is kept only in your kernel. Nothing saves or serves it. See Saving, MLflow and training in the database."},"528":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"`nb.use_model` and `nb.forget_model` look like they are about a model, but they are about the AI model endpoint and the Langfuse keys. They do not save or delete a trained model."},"529":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"Scoring a model inside the database is the ML Engineer's path. It needs the udf-devkit add-on (`exakit marketplace udf-devkit`), which provides a Python container for training and scoring."},"530":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"`exakit model put <file>` copies a model file into the bucket folder that the database's functions read, and `exakit model list` shows what is there. `nb.model_list()` returns the same list in a notebook."},"531":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"The ML Engineer's training runs `nb.prepare_training`, which is described on the model page. It writes the coefficients of a trained logistic regression beside the training hand-off, so a scoring notebook can read them without MLflow."},"532":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"If you need a model in production, that path is the one the kit has. The Data Scientist notebooks stop at the result, the dashboard and the hand-off."},"533":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model#deploying-a-model","content":"Back to Communicate and deploy."},"534":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","type":"page","content":"The hand-off bundle","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle"},"535":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","content":"The folder and zip that let a colleague repeat your work, what each file holds, and how to rerun it."},"536":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#the-hand-off-bundle","content":"The hand-off bundle"},"537":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"What is in the bundle"},"538":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#writing-it","content":"Writing it"},"539":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#repeating-the-work","content":"Repeating the work"},"540":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#the-hand-off-bundle","content":"`nb.handoff_bundle(connection, dataset)` writes one folder to `~/.exasol-starter-kit/bundles/<workflow id>/`, with a zip beside it. It needs no Dash Server and holds no password. The folder is named after the workflow. A bundle made with no workflow is named after the dataset."},"541":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"File"},"542":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"What it is"},"543":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"`README.md`"},"544":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"What the data is, which column map version the numbers rest on, which questions were approved, which dashboards were previewed or published, which model traces were made, and how to repeat the work"},"545":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"`descriptor.yaml`"},"546":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"The approved column map, copied from the kit's store"},"547":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"`schema-map.json`"},"548":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"The approved schema map, when the schema has one"},"549":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"`queries.sql`"},"550":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"Every distinct query the workflow ran, each with its SHA-256 and row count"},"551":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"`workflow.jsonl`"},"552":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"The workflow log, as it is"},"553":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#what-is-in-the-bundle","content":"For `amazon_sales`, the folder holds the approved column map, the queries the workflow ran, the workflow log and the README."},"554":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#writing-it","content":"Running it again for the same workflow replaces the folder and the zip. It is a snapshot. Nothing in it checks itself against the data later. The page on A training hand-off that went stale describes the one check the kit makes, and the bundle has no such check."},"555":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#repeating-the-work","content":"A colleague follows the README:"},"556":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#repeating-the-work","content":"Load the same table. For a kit dataset, run `exakit data-load <id>`."},"557":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#repeating-the-work","content":"Open the approved column map with `nb.dataset(connection, \"<id>\")`, or `exakit describe <id> --show`."},"558":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#repeating-the-work","content":"Run the statements in `queries.sql` in order."},"559":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle#repeating-the-work","content":"Back to Communicate and deploy. Next: The training hand-off."},"560":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","type":"page","content":"Communicate and deploy","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate"},"561":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate","content":"Turn one checked result into a dashboard that is previewed, checked and published, then pack the work into one folder a colleague can repeat."},"562":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate#the-flow","content":"The flow"},"563":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"Where you do it"},"564":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate#how-we-used-it","content":"How we used it"},"565":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"In this stage"},"566":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"What you have at the end"},"567":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate","content":"Communicating a result means two things: showing the figures to people who will not open the notebook, and leaving enough behind that someone else can repeat the work. The kit does both. A dashboard goes up on Dash Server, a kit add-on that the notebook installs and starts for you. It goes up as a preview first, and only a person can publish it. The hand-off bundle is one folder and a zip, and it needs no server. The kit does not deploy a model from these notebooks. The page on deploying a model explains what exists and where it lives."},"568":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"Guided view"},"569":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"Notebook code"},"570":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"Terminal"},"571":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"`03_share`: the **Share** card has the steps Dash Server, Preview, Publish and Hand off."},"572":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"`03_share`: `nb.share`, `dashboard_draft.preview`, `dashboard_draft.confirm`, `nb.handoff_bundle`. `nb.dash_tools()` connects to Dash Server."},"573":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"`exakit marketplace dash-server` installs the add-on. `exakit start dash-server` starts it. `exakit status --json` gives its address. `exakit workflow show <id>` lists the workflow's steps. The bundle has no command of its own."},"574":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#where-you-do-it","content":"The publish step is off by default in `03_share`. `FIGURES_CHECKED = False` stops the publish. The cell tells you to \"open the healthy preview and check the figures before promoting.\" Set it to `True` only after you have checked the preview."},"575":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#how-we-used-it","content":"The worked example is `amazon_sales`, a result that counts order lines by category. The notebook builds the query from the approved roles, previews it, and writes the hand-off folder. The steps are on Build the dashboard query."},"576":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#how-we-used-it","content":"For `amazon_sales` the query is `SELECT CATEGORY AS CATEGORY, COUNT(*) AS ORDER_LINES FROM AMAZON.ORDER_LINES GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY ORDER_LINES DESC`. The descriptor records nine distinct values for `CATEGORY`. The chart groups the smallest categories as Other when more than eight rows are shown, so check that grouped figure against the SQL as well."},"577":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Build the dashboard query\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/dashboard-query\" description=\"The query the notebook builds from the roles, and its title and name\" />"},"578":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Preview, check, publish\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish\" description=\"The order the code enforces, and what each step checks\" />"},"579":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"What a reader sees\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees\" description=\"The filters, charts and table on the dashboard page\" />"},"580":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"The hand-off bundle\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/hand-off-bundle\" description=\"The folder and zip a colleague uses to repeat the work\" />"},"581":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"The training hand-off\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off\" description=\"The file the ML Engineer's training reads, and its split rule\" />"},"582":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Deploying a model\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/deploying-a-model\" description=\"What the kit does and does not deploy\" />"},"583":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"When it goes wrong\" href=\"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting\" description=\"Dash Server, workflow and publish problems, and their fixes\" />"},"584":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"A dashboard, first as a preview and then, after you check it, as a live address that you keep with the workflow id. You also have one hand-off folder and its zip. The next step is to notice when the data, the column map or the model changes: Monitor and iterate."},"585":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"page","content":"Preview, check, publish","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish"},"586":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","content":"The three steps that take a dashboard from a draft to a live address, the checks the code makes at each step, and what a person must do."},"587":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview-check-publish","content":"Preview, check, publish"},"588":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#before-you-preview-dash-server","content":"Before you preview: Dash Server"},"589":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"Preview"},"590":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#check","content":"Check"},"591":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#publish","content":"Publish"},"592":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#the-workflow-log","content":"The workflow log"},"593":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview-check-publish","content":"The code enforces the order. You cannot skip a step."},"594":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#before-you-preview-dash-server","content":"The notebook calls `nb.dash_tools()`, which starts Dash Server if it is stopped. Dash Server is a kit add-on. The add-on is installed with `exakit marketplace dash-server`. The add-ons page explains how add-ons work."},"595":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"`preview` checks four things before it creates anything:"},"596":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"that the workflow is the one the dashboard was made in"},"597":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"that the read-only identity is validated"},"598":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"that Dash Server's profile is the kit's read-only user on the kit's database"},"599":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"that the dashboard's name is not already taken by a dashboard that belongs to another dataset, or that has no readable lineage"},"600":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#preview","content":"Then it creates the preview, confirms that the built copy holds the approved SQL and lineage files unchanged, and runs a health check. A preview that fails the health check is marked unhealthy, and the kit will not publish it."},"601":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#check","content":"Open the preview and compare the figures with the SQL. Use the page described on What a reader sees. The chart's grouped Other figure is one to check against the query. Only a person can make this check. The notebook never does it for you."},"602":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#publish","content":"`FIGURES_CHECKED` is `False` in `03_share`. Set it to `True` after the check, and run the cell. In code:"},"603":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#publish","content":"`confirm` refuses unless `figures_checked=True` and the preview is healthy. It promotes that exact revision, starts the app if it is not mounted, and checks the live address. If the live check fails, the error says to roll back in Dash Server."},"604":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#publish","content":"In automated runs the check is never set, so nothing is published without a person."},"605":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#the-workflow-log","content":"Each preview and each publish adds a line to the workflow log, with the query's digest, the descriptor version and the revision number. The kinds are listed on The workflow log."},"606":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/preview-check-publish#the-workflow-log","content":"Back to Communicate and deploy. Next: What a reader sees."},"607":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","type":"page","content":"The training hand-off","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off"},"608":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","content":"The file that passes a feature view, a target and a split from the Data Scientist's work to the ML Engineer's training, and the rules for its split."},"609":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-training-hand-off","content":"The training hand-off"},"610":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-split","content":"The split"},"611":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#when-the-hand-off-goes-stale","content":"When the hand-off goes stale"},"612":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-training-hand-off","content":"The training hand-off is the ML Engineer's file. It is one JSON file with the feature view, the target, the split and the training SQL. The Data Scientist notebooks do not write it. The ML Engineer's notebook writes one if none exists."},"613":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-training-hand-off","content":"`nb.write_handoff(conn, ds)` writes the file to `~/.exasol-starter-kit/handoffs/<id>-v<n>.json`, where `<n>` is the descriptor's version. `nb.read_handoff` reads it back. The file names a table, columns, SQL and digests. It never names a host, a user or a password."},"614":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-split","content":"Two rules decide how the rows are split for training:"},"615":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-split","content":"**A date in the column map gives a time split.** This is the default. The oldest rows train the model and the newest rows test it."},"616":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-split","content":"**No date means no time split.** The kit refuses a time split that the data cannot support, and names the fix. A dataset with no date, such as `fraud_transactions`, needs a split named on purpose: `nb.write_handoff(conn, ds, split=\"random\")`."},"617":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#the-split","content":"The kit does not choose a random split for you. `split=\"random\"` is a reproducible hash of each row's role columns, so the same rows always give the same split."},"618":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#when-the-hand-off-goes-stale","content":"A hand-off that no longer matches the column map or the data is refused. The check and the fix are on A training hand-off that went stale."},"619":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/training-hand-off#when-the-hand-off-goes-stale","content":"Back to Communicate and deploy. Next: Deploying a model."},"620":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting"},"621":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"Fixes for Dash Server, workflow, preview and publish problems, and for the hand-off."},"622":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"Each entry quotes the message or describes the step that stops."},"623":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The notebook installs and starts it when you call `nb.dash_tools()` or `nb.addon_ready(\"dash-server\")`. To do it yourself, run `exakit marketplace dash-server`, then `exakit start dash-server`, then `exakit status` to confirm. See Add-ons."},"624":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The dashboard belongs to the workflow it was made in. Run `nb.continue_workflow(\"share\")` in this notebook, or resume the workflow with `nb.workflow(resume=\"wf-...\")`, and try again."},"625":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The preview needs the kit's read-only user to be validated. Run `exakit mcp-doctor` to check it. When the message says the profile must validate as the read-only user, run `exakit mcp-setup`, then restart Dash Server."},"626":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The error reads \"choose another APP_NAME\". The name is taken by a dashboard the kit did not make, or one made for another dataset. Choose another `APP_NAME`. The kit never overwrites a dashboard that belongs to a different dataset."},"627":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The health check failed, so the kit will not publish it. The error reads \"Dash Server preview is unhealthy\". Open the Dash Server log with `exakit logs dash-server`, fix the cause, and run the preview again."},"628":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"`confirm` was called without `figures_checked=True`, or the preview is not healthy. Check the figures against the SQL, then confirm again. See Preview, check, publish."},"629":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The error reads \"use app_rollback in Dash Server\". The published revision is not healthy. Roll back there, then check the preview again before you publish another revision."},"630":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"The training hand-off no longer matches the column map or the data. Read the line the kit prints, then write the hand-off again with `nb.write_handoff(conn, ds)`. See A training hand-off that went stale."},"631":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/troubleshooting","content":"Back to Communicate and deploy."},"632":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","type":"page","content":"What a reader sees","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Communicate and deploy"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees"},"633":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","content":"The filters, charts and table on a dashboard page, and what each part shows."},"634":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#what-a-reader-sees","content":"What a reader sees"},"635":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#check-the-figures-against-the-sql","content":"Check the figures against the SQL"},"636":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#what-a-reader-sees","content":"The dashboard is a view of the reviewed SQL result. It is not a new calculation over the table. Its page says so."},"637":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#what-a-reader-sees","content":"**Filters.** Categories, a Show setting (10 by default), Sort by, and Chart layout (horizontal or vertical bars). Top N applies after the filters. **Reset filters** clears them."},"638":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#what-a-reader-sees","content":"**Category comparison.** A bar chart of the values in the current selection."},"639":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#what-a-reader-sees","content":"**Composition.** A share-of-total chart. The kit draws it only when you pass `measure=\"count\"`, because a total of a measure that is not a count has no meaning. With more than eight rows shown, the note says smaller categories are grouped as Other."},"640":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#what-a-reader-sees","content":"**Behind the charts.** The same filtered rows as a table, sortable, with **Export CSV**."},"641":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#check-the-figures-against-the-sql","content":"When you check a preview, compare three things with the query on Build the dashboard query:"},"642":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#check-the-figures-against-the-sql","content":"The category names in the table match the values in the query's first column."},"643":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#check-the-figures-against-the-sql","content":"The counts match the counts the query returns. `nb.show_sql()` prints the last query the notebook ran."},"644":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#check-the-figures-against-the-sql","content":"The composition chart's Other group equals the sum of the categories it groups."},"645":{"id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/communicate/what-a-reader-sees#check-the-figures-against-the-sql","content":"Back to Preview, check, publish. Next: The hand-off bundle."},"646":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","type":"page","content":"The checks","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks"},"647":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","content":"The functions that check the data and the model before you rely on a score, and what each one reports."},"648":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#the-checks","content":"The checks"},"649":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#what-to-do-with-a-finding","content":"What to do with a finding"},"650":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","content":"Three checks sit behind the evaluation. The first two print what they find and return it as a value. The third returns true or false."},"651":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#the-checks","content":"**`nb.leakage_check`** lists the columns that give the answer away. It tests each column that is not the target, a key, an identifier or free text. The approval runs it too. See Leakage."},"652":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#the-checks","content":"**`nb.data_checks`** reports repeated keys and ids, exact copies of a row, empty cells, placeholder values such as `Unknown Device`, and a rare label. The repeated keys and copies can inflate a score when a copied row lands on both sides of the split. The data checks are documented on the data checks reference page."},"653":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#the-checks","content":"**`nb.check_model_compatibility(conn, model_version, alias=\"PYTHON3\", model_path=\"model.pkl\")`** checks a pickled scikit-learn model before anything is scored in the database. It refuses when the model's scikit-learn version differs from the version in the database's Python container. The Data Scientist notebooks do not pickle or score models, so this check belongs to the ML Engineer's scoring notebook."},"654":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#what-to-do-with-a-finding","content":"Each finding says what it does to a result. The kit does not fix the table or drop rows for you. Decide what to do, make the change in your own cleaned view or column map, and run the check again. The cleaned views are on Prepare."},"655":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks#what-to-do-with-a-finding","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: When it goes wrong."},"656":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","type":"page","content":"Comparing models","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models"},"657":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","content":"What the kit does not test when it compares models, the numbers it gives you to judge a difference, and how to compute a p-value yourself."},"658":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#what-you-have-to-judge-a-difference","content":"What you have to judge a difference"},"659":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#if-you-need-a-p-value-or-an-interval","content":"If you need a p-value or an interval"},"660":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","content":"The kit does not compute a p-value, a confidence interval or a significance test. A p-value is a number that says how likely a gap this size would be by chance alone. A confidence interval is a range the true score probably falls in. The kit gives you the parts you need to judge a difference yourself."},"661":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#what-you-have-to-judge-a-difference","content":"**The spread across folds.** `cv_sd` on the board shows how much a model's score moves between folds. A gap between two models that is smaller than that spread is not a reliable difference."},"662":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#what-you-have-to-judge-a-difference","content":"**The model rule.** The lab picks the simpler model when two are within 0.01 of each other. That is a rule for choosing, not a test that the gap is real. See The models the lab tries."},"663":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#what-you-have-to-judge-a-difference","content":"**The feature rule.** A verdict of `helps` needs a rise of 0.005 in cross-validation and no fall of more than 0.002 on the held-out rows. That is a rule of thumb. See Feature ideas."},"664":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#what-you-have-to-judge-a-difference","content":"**Small groups.** Groups with fewer than 30 rows are not compared. This applies to the group and trend notes. See The readout notes."},"665":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#what-you-have-to-judge-a-difference","content":"**The held-out size.** The warnings for a small held-out set are on Hold-out."},"666":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#if-you-need-a-p-value-or-an-interval","content":"Compute it in your own code, on the rows you have, and say in your write-up that the kit did not compute it. `cv` is the mean of the fold scores and `cv_sd` is their standard deviation, so the two are a starting point. They are not a full analysis."},"667":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models#if-you-need-a-p-value-or-an-interval","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: The readout notes."},"668":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","type":"page","content":"Hold-out","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out"},"669":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","content":"How the kit keeps training rows and test rows apart, and why the notebook baseline and the lab split in different ways."},"670":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"Hold-out: how rows are kept apart"},"671":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-baselines-score-on-amazon_sales","content":"The baseline's score on amazon_sales"},"672":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-labs-hold-out-on-amazon_sales","content":"The lab's hold-out on amazon_sales"},"673":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-hold-out-on-fraud_transactions","content":"The hold-out on fraud_transactions"},"674":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","content":"Every model in the lab is trained on the same training rows and scored on the same held-out rows. The held-out rows are the test. Keeping them apart is the only way to see how a model does on rows it has not seen."},"675":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"Split method"},"676":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"When the lab uses it"},"677":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"What the held-out rows are"},"678":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"`time`"},"679":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"The default when the column map has a date"},"680":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"The newest rows. The model learns from the oldest and is tested on the newest, so it never sees the future. Cross-validation uses time-ordered folds (`TimeSeriesSplit`)."},"681":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"`random`"},"682":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"The default when there is no date"},"683":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"A random share, with a fixed seed. For yes or no it is stratified, so both sides keep the same share of 1s."},"684":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"`group`"},"685":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"You name a group column with `split_group`"},"686":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"Whole groups. No group appears on both sides."},"687":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"Two settings shape the split. `train_pct` is the share used for training: 50 to 90, and 75 by default. `folds` is the number of cross-validation folds on the training side: 5 by default."},"688":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"The kit warns when the held-out set is too small to trust:"},"689":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"For yes or no: fewer than 30 rows with a 1 held out."},"690":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#hold-out-how-rows-are-kept-apart","content":"For a number to predict: fewer than 100 rows held out."},"691":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-baselines-score-on-amazon_sales","content":"The notebook holds out a quarter of the rows, scores the model on them, and prints the note:"},"692":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-baselines-score-on-amazon_sales","content":"The notebook's hold-out is a stratified random split with `random_state=0`. The same rows are held out on every rerun, as long as the table has not changed."},"693":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-baselines-score-on-amazon_sales","content":"The notebook baseline splits at random, even though `amazon_sales` has a date. The lab splits by time. The two scores are measured on different rows, so do not compare them as if they were one test."},"694":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-labs-hold-out-on-amazon_sales","content":"The lab reads the newest 50,000 rows, since the table has 128,975. It learns from the oldest 75% of those and is tested on the newest 25%. The output line reads: \"The oldest 75% by ORDER_DATE to learn from, the newest 25% to test on. The model never sees the future.\""},"695":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-hold-out-on-fraud_transactions","content":"With no date, the lab holds out rows at random, stratified so both sides keep the same share of fraud. The table has 50,000 distinct `TRANSACTION_ID` values across 51,000 rows, so 1,000 ids repeat. A repeated row that lands on both sides of the split can make the score look better than it is. `nb.data_checks` reports repeated ids and exact copies. Read that report before you trust a score on this table."},"696":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out#the-hold-out-on-fraud_transactions","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: Metrics."},"697":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","type":"page","content":"Evaluate","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate"},"698":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate","content":"Score the model on rows it did not learn from, read the metric for your task, and check the traps that make a score look better than it is."},"699":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"Where you do it"},"700":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#how-we-used-it","content":"How we used it"},"701":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"In this stage"},"702":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"What you have at the end"},"703":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate","content":"Evaluation answers one question: how well will this model do on rows it has not seen? A score on the rows it learned from says little, because a model can memorise them. The kit holds rows out before it trains, reports the metric that fits your task, and warns you about the traps it can detect: columns that give the answer away, rare answers, and held-out sets that are too small. It does not run significance tests. The page on comparing models says what to do about that."},"704":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"Guided view"},"705":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"Notebook code"},"706":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"Terminal"},"707":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"`01_explore`: the **Build a model** card runs the same lab, and its results show the leaderboard and the pick."},"708":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"The cells **Score on unseen rows** and **The model lab (optional)** in `01_explore`. `lab.board`, `lab.importance` and `lab.note` hold the results."},"709":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#where-you-do-it","content":"None. Scoring happens in the notebook."},"710":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#how-we-used-it","content":"On `amazon_sales`, the notebook baseline is scored on a quarter of the rows it did not train on, with ROC AUC. The notebook prints the score. This repository does not record a value for it, so these pages quote none."},"711":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#how-we-used-it","content":"Two results are measured on the real database and are the basis of the warnings:"},"712":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#how-we-used-it","content":"**The leakage trap.** `STATUS` is `Cancelled` on exactly the 18,332 rows whose `CANCELLED` is 1. A model that reads `STATUS` learns the answer it was given. See Leakage."},"713":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#how-we-used-it","content":"**The rare answer.** `fraud_transactions` has 2,510 fraud rows out of 51,000 (4.9%). A model that always says \"not fraud\" is about 95% accurate, so accuracy is the wrong score. See Rare answers."},"714":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Hold-out: how rows are kept apart\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/hold-out\" description=\"The time, random and group splits, and the warnings for a small held-out set\" />"},"715":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Metrics\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics\" description=\"The score each task uses, and what the board's columns mean\" />"},"716":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Reading ROC AUC\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc\" description=\"What the number means, and what the note says for each range\" />"},"717":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Rare answers\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers\" description=\"Why accuracy fails when the answer is rare, and which score the kit uses instead\" />"},"718":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Leakage\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage\" description=\"Columns that give the answer away, how the kit finds them, and how to check\" />"},"719":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Comparing models\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/comparing-models\" description=\"What the kit does not test, and what to do about a difference\" />"},"720":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"The readout notes\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes\" description=\"The plain-language notes under each result\" />"},"721":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"The checks\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/checks\" description=\"The functions that check the data and the model before you rely on them\" />"},"722":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"When it goes wrong\" href=\"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting\" description=\"Warnings and suspicious scores, and what to do about them\" />"},"723":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"A score on rows the model did not learn from, what that score means in words, the list of what the model leans on, and the warnings the kit raised. The score shows that the inputs carry signal about the outcome. It does not show that any input causes it. Next, share the result in Communicate and deploy."},"724":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","type":"page","content":"Leakage","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage"},"725":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","content":"Columns that give the answer away, the measured case on amazon_sales, how the kit finds and leaves them out, and how to check your own data."},"726":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#the-case-on-amazon_sales","content":"The case on amazon_sales"},"727":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"How the kit finds it"},"728":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-kit-does-with-a-flagged-column","content":"What the kit does with a flagged column"},"729":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#check-it-yourself","content":"Check it yourself"},"730":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"What the check cannot see"},"731":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","content":"Leakage means a column carries the answer. Its values are written after the outcome happens, so a model that reads it learns the answer it was given, not a pattern that works on new rows. A model with leakage looks excellent on the rows it learned from and fails on new rows."},"732":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#the-case-on-amazon_sales","content":"`STATUS` is the classic trap. The dataset README says `STATUS`, `COURIER_STATUS`, `QTY` and `AMOUNT` all change once an order is cancelled. So a cancelled order says so in `STATUS`, and the target `CANCELLED` is 1 for that order."},"733":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#the-case-on-amazon_sales","content":"This was measured on the sample table, `AMAZON.ORDER_LINES`, on 12 November 2026. The queries are read-only:"},"734":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#the-case-on-amazon_sales","content":"The three counts are equal. Every row with `CANCELLED` = 1 has `STATUS` = `Cancelled`, and no other row does. So `STATUS` gives the target away completely. A model that reads it will look perfect."},"735":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#the-case-on-amazon_sales","content":"The notebook's comment puts the problem this way: such a column \"predicts the target perfectly on old rows and fails on new rows.\""},"736":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"`nb.leakage_check` flags a column when all of these hold:"},"737":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"It has 2 to 50 distinct values. Columns with more values are not tested."},"738":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"Each of its values is nearly always 1 (at least 99% of its rows) or nearly always 0 (at least 99%)."},"739":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"Those values together cover at least 95% of all rows, and they hold at least 90% of the 1s."},"740":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"For `STATUS`, the value `Cancelled` has a 1 in every row, and every other status has a 0 in every row. So every value is pure, the pure values cover all the rows, and they hold all the 1s. The rule flags `STATUS`."},"741":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#how-the-kit-finds-it","content":"Its message, as `nb.leakage_check` prints it, has this form:"},"742":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-kit-does-with-a-flagged-column","content":"The kit guards against leakage in three places."},"743":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-kit-does-with-a-flagged-column","content":"**The approval warns.** When you approve the column map, the card says `STATUS` gives the answer away, because it is filled in after the outcome, and that the model step leaves it out."},"744":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-kit-does-with-a-flagged-column","content":"**The baseline never reads it.** The notebook baseline skips a flagged category or entity. On `amazon_sales` it reads only the category, the state and the month."},"745":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-kit-does-with-a-flagged-column","content":"**The lab leaves it out.** `lab_data.left_out` lists `STATUS` with the reason \"gives the answer away\"."},"746":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-kit-does-with-a-flagged-column","content":"The quickstart says the check flags `STATUS` on `amazon_sales`. Confirm that on your data, because the check depends on the values present in your table."},"747":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"The check tests only columns with 2 to 50 distinct values. A numeric column such as `AMOUNT` usually has many more values, so the check skips it. The README says `AMOUNT` changes after a cancellation, so the check cannot be the last word on it."},"748":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"Two other checks cover the gap:"},"749":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"**Look at `lab.importance` after every run.** If a column you suspect is at the top, it is a lead to read."},"750":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"**Leave it out with `leaks=`.** For example: `leaks = nb.leakage_check(connection, dataset) + [\"AMOUNT\"]`, then pass `leaks=leaks` to `nb.model_lab`. The model lab page has the full example."},"751":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"For a number to predict, the lab also drops any measure whose correlation with the target is above 0.98, because it moves with the target almost exactly."},"752":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/leakage#what-the-check-cannot-see","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: Comparing models."},"753":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","type":"page","content":"Metrics","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics"},"754":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","content":"The score each task uses, the other scores the board shows, and what the board's columns mean."},"755":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Metrics"},"756":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#the-columns-on-the-board","content":"The columns on the board"},"757":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","content":"Each model is scored with the metric for its task. The metric the lab picks by comes first in the table. The board shows the other columns as well."},"758":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Task"},"759":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"The metric the lab picks by"},"760":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Also on the held-out rows"},"761":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Predict yes or no"},"762":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"ROC AUC. PR AUC when the rarer answer is under 5%."},"763":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"ROC AUC, PR AUC and F1 at a 0.5 cut-off"},"764":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Predict a number"},"765":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"R squared"},"766":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"R squared, mean absolute error (MAE) and root mean squared error (RMSE)"},"767":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Find natural groups"},"768":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Silhouette score: how well each row fits its own group compared with the next one"},"769":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"The number of groups tried, and the score for each"},"770":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"Find unusual rows"},"771":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"None is scored. You set the share to flag (`contamination`, 2% by default)."},"772":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"The flagged count, the share, and what sets the flagged rows apart"},"773":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"In plain words:"},"774":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"**ROC AUC** is the chance that the model gives a row with a 1 a higher score than a row with a 0. It is explained on Reading ROC AUC."},"775":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"**PR AUC** is the average precision score: how well the model's top-ranked rows contain the rare 1s. It is the one to read when 1s are rare."},"776":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"**F1** combines how many flagged rows are right and how many real 1s are found, at one cut-off (0.5 here)."},"777":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"**R squared** is the share of the spread in the number that the model explains. A value of 0 means no better than the average."},"778":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"**MAE** is the average size of the miss, in the number's own units. **RMSE** is similar, but it punishes large misses more."},"779":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#metrics","content":"**Silhouette score** is how close each row sits to its own group, compared with the nearest other group."},"780":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#the-columns-on-the-board","content":"`cv` is the mean cross-validated score for the metric the lab picks by."},"781":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#the-columns-on-the-board","content":"`cv_sd` is how much that score varies across folds. Read it before you compare two models."},"782":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-28","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#the-columns-on-the-board","content":"`held` is the held-out score for the same metric."},"783":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-29","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#the-columns-on-the-board","content":"The board's other columns (`roc_auc`, `pr_auc`, `f1`, `r2`, `mae`, `rmse`) are the held-out values for each model."},"784":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics-30","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/metrics#the-columns-on-the-board","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: Reading ROC AUC."},"785":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","type":"page","content":"Rare answers","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers"},"786":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","content":"Why accuracy misleads when the answer is rare, the two thresholds the kit uses for a rare answer, and the score it uses instead."},"787":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#why-accuracy-misleads","content":"Why accuracy misleads"},"788":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Two thresholds, for two different jobs"},"789":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#what-pr-auc-is","content":"What PR AUC is"},"790":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#what-the-readout-note-says","content":"What the readout note says"},"791":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#repeated-rows-can-also-inflate-a-rare-answer-score","content":"Repeated rows can also inflate a rare-answer score"},"792":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","content":"When one answer is rare, a model can look good by always giving the common answer. This page shows the numbers from the two sample datasets, and what the kit does about it."},"793":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#why-accuracy-misleads","content":"`fraud_transactions` has 51,000 rows, and 2,510 of them are fraud (4.9%). A model that always says \"not fraud\" is right on 48,490 rows, which is about 95% accuracy. It finds no fraud at all. A score that rewards that model is useless for finding fraud."},"794":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#why-accuracy-misleads","content":"The data check reports the same thing. `nb.data_checks` prints a finding named \"rare label\", and the sentence says what a model that always answers the common value achieves."},"795":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"The kit uses two cut-offs for the share of the rarer answer. They do different jobs, so the numbers differ."},"796":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Where"},"797":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Threshold"},"798":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"What happens"},"799":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"`amazon_sales` (14.2%)"},"800":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"`fraud_transactions` (4.9%)"},"801":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"`nb.data_checks` (the rare-label finding)"},"802":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Under 10%"},"803":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"The label is reported as rare"},"804":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Not reported"},"805":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Reported as rare"},"806":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"The model lab"},"807":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"Under 5%"},"808":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"The lab scores with PR AUC instead of ROC AUC"},"809":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"ROC AUC"},"810":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#two-thresholds-for-two-different-jobs","content":"PR AUC"},"811":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#what-pr-auc-is","content":"PR AUC is the average precision score. It ranks the rows by the model's score and asks how many of the rows at the top are real 1s, averaged over the ranking. It is only interested in finding the 1s, so it does not reward a model for being right about the many 0s. The lab records the reason in `lab.run.notes`: \"The 1s are rare, so the score is PR AUC, which focuses on finding them.\""},"812":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#what-the-readout-note-says","content":"For ROC AUC, `nb.note_model` names the always-common guess and its accuracy as the baseline to beat. The note also says that accuracy is not the score when the answer is rare."},"813":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#repeated-rows-can-also-inflate-a-rare-answer-score","content":"`fraud_transactions` has 51,000 rows and 50,000 distinct `TRANSACTION_ID` values. So 1,000 ids appear twice. When a repeated copy lands on both sides of the split, the model can score a row it has already learned. `nb.data_checks` reports repeated ids and exact copies. Read that report before you judge a score on this table. The split is on Hold-out."},"814":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers-28","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/rare-answers#repeated-rows-can-also-inflate-a-rare-answer-score","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: Leakage."},"815":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","type":"page","content":"The readout notes","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes"},"816":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","content":"The plain-language notes the readout helpers print under a result, what each one says, and where the words come from."},"817":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#the-readout-notes","content":"The readout notes"},"818":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"Where the words come from"},"819":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","content":"The readout helpers print a plain-language note under each result and return it as text. Nothing is computed that the result does not hold. If a note would need a number the result lacks, it leaves the sentence out."},"820":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#the-readout-notes","content":"**`nb.note_groups`** is for a per-group result. It says what one row is, the overall figure, the highest and the lowest group, which groups have too few rows to trust (fewer than 30), and that a difference between groups is not a cause."},"821":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#the-readout-notes","content":"**`nb.note_trend`** is for a per-period result. It names the busiest period and the highest and lowest percentage, leaves out periods with fewer than 30 rows, and says that a change over time shows when something moved, not why."},"822":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#the-readout-notes","content":"**`nb.note_model`** is for the small model's score. It says what the score means in words (the ranges are on Reading ROC AUC), how many rows the model learned from, and the baseline to beat."},"823":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#the-readout-notes","content":"The 30-row limit is one constant, `SMALL`, in `readout.py`. It is the same for groups and periods."},"824":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"The notes take their words from the column map. The grain \"one row per order line\" makes the note say \"order lines\", and the count column is named `ORDER_LINES`. Three helpers do that naming:"},"825":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"`nb.name` gives a column's readable name."},"826":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"`nb.count_name` gives the name of the count column for the dataset."},"827":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"`nb.noun` gives the plural noun for one row."},"828":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"The note is printed under the table and also returned as a string, so you can copy it next to the figures in a report."},"829":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/readout-notes#where-the-words-come-from","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: The checks."},"830":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","type":"page","content":"Reading ROC AUC","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc"},"831":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","content":"What the ROC AUC number means in plain words, and what the kit's note says for each range of the score."},"832":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"Reading ROC AUC"},"833":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#what-the-lab-does-with-it","content":"What the lab does with it"},"834":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#where-the-note-comes-from","content":"Where the note comes from"},"835":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","content":"ROC AUC is the score the lab uses for a yes-or-no prediction when the rarer answer is 5% or more. It is a number from 0 to 1."},"836":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"ROC AUC is the chance that the model gives a row with a 1 a higher score than a row with a 0. Pick one row that has a 1 and one that has a 0 at random. A score of 0.5 means the model ranks them correctly only half the time, which is guessing. A score of 1.0 means it always ranks the 1 higher."},"837":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"The readout note turns the number into words:"},"838":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"ROC AUC"},"839":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"What the note says"},"840":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"Below 0.6"},"841":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"About as good as guessing"},"842":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"0.6 to below 0.7"},"843":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"A weak signal"},"844":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"0.7 to below 0.85"},"845":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"A useful signal"},"846":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"0.85 and above"},"847":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"A strong signal. Check that it is not too good to be true."},"848":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#reading-roc-auc","content":"The note also says that 50% is guessing, and that accuracy is not the score when the answer is rare."},"849":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#what-the-lab-does-with-it","content":"The lab's own `baseline` model always gives the same answer, so its ROC AUC is 0.5. Any real model should score above it."},"850":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#what-the-lab-does-with-it","content":"The lab scores each model by cross-validated ROC AUC and picks from those scores (the rule is on The models the lab tries). It reports the held-out ROC AUC beside each one."},"851":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#what-the-lab-does-with-it","content":"On `amazon_sales`, the rarer answer is 14.2% of rows, so the metric is ROC AUC. On `fraud_transactions`, the rarer answer is 4.9%, so the metric is PR AUC instead. See Rare answers."},"852":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#where-the-note-comes-from","content":"`nb.note_model` writes the note. Its wording is in `readout.py`, in the function of the same name. The ranges in the table above are the ones coded there. Nothing in the note is a statistical test."},"853":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/roc-auc#where-the-note-comes-from","content":"Back to Evaluate. Next: Rare answers."},"854":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Evaluate"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting"},"855":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"The warnings and suspicious scores you can meet while you evaluate a model, what each one means, and what to do."},"856":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"Each entry quotes the warning or describes the score you see."},"857":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"The warning reads \"Only N rows with a 1 are held out, so the held-out score is shaky\". It appears when fewer than 30 rows with a 1 are held out. Lower `train_pct` (50 is the lowest it accepts), so more rows are held out, or use more data. See Hold-out."},"858":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"The error reads \"One side of the split has only one answer, so no score can be computed\". No score can be computed when one side has only 0s or only 1s. Change the split to `split_method=\"random\"`, which is stratified for yes or no."},"859":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"The idea you tried scored above 0.98 on the held-out rows, and the verdict is `suspicious`. The kit says \"The score jumped almost to perfect. That usually means the idea gives the answer away. Don't keep it.\" Do not keep it. For the whole model, run `nb.leakage_check` and read `lab_data.left_out`. See Leakage."},"860":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"Treat it as a leak until proved otherwise. Check the importance list, the leakage check and the column map. A score that is near perfect on the first run is almost never the model's real skill. The leakage page shows how a column like `STATUS` produces one."},"861":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"A gap in either direction can be noise, especially with a small held-out set. Look at the number of rows held out, and at `cv_sd`, before you read anything into the gap. See Comparing models."},"862":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"On `fraud_transactions`, 1,000 ids appear twice. A copy on both sides of the split can make the held-out score look better than it is. Run `nb.data_checks` and read the repeated-id finding. See Rare answers."},"863":{"id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/evaluate/troubleshooting","content":"Back to Evaluate."},"864":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","type":"page","content":"Add a command","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command"},"865":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","content":"Add an exakit.nb helper, wire it to a card, and get its documentation for free."},"866":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#write-the-helper","content":"Write the helper"},"867":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#put-it-in-a-notebook","content":"Put it in a notebook"},"868":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#give-a-card-a-button-for-it","content":"Give a card a button for it"},"869":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#regenerate","content":"Regenerate"},"870":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#test","content":"Test"},"871":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#write-the-helper","content":"Add a function to a module in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/`, with a docstring. The first sentence becomes its one-line summary here; the rest becomes its reference text. Indented lines in a docstring are shown as code."},"872":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#write-the-helper","content":"Export it: import it in `exakit_nb/__init__.py` and add its name to `__all__`."},"873":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#put-it-in-a-notebook","content":"Add the call to the code section of the notebook it belongs to, guarded by a variable when it is optional:"},"874":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#give-a-card-a-button-for-it","content":"Add one entry to `actions.ACTIONS` in `exakit_guide/actions.py`: the code and the notebook it belongs to. Have the screen call `actions.run(...)`."},"875":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#regenerate","content":"The new helper now has an entry in its module's reference page and a row in All commands. `nb.docs(nb.your_helper)` opens it once the site is rebuilt."},"876":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/add-a-command#test","content":"A whole new module gets its own reference page automatically, titled from its file name. Give it a proper title and a place in the order by adding one line to `PAGES` in `tools/nb_docs.py`."},"877":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"page","content":"These docs","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site"},"878":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","content":"How this documentation is written, built and shipped, and how to change a page."},"879":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"880":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#change-a-page","content":"Change a page"},"881":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#edit","content":"Edit"},"882":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#preview-while-you-write","content":"Preview while you write"},"883":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#build-and-publish","content":"Build and publish"},"884":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#check","content":"Check"},"885":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#pages-you-do-not-edit","content":"Pages you do not edit"},"886":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#links","content":"Links"},"887":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#how-it-works","content":"**Source:** MDX pages in `notebook/docs/content/docs/`, one folder per persona. The folder layout is the sidebar; each folder's `meta.json` sets its title and page order, and a persona's folder is marked `\"root\": true`, which makes it an entry in the list at the top of the sidebar."},"888":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#how-it-works","content":"**Build:** a static site built with Node on a maintainer's machine. Users never need Node."},"889":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#how-it-works","content":"**Shipped:** the built files, in `notebook/docs/site/`, travel with the kit."},"890":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#how-it-works","content":"**Served:** `nb.docs()` serves that folder from the kit's own Python, on this computer only."},"891":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#how-it-works","content":"Search runs in the browser on an index inside the site. It forgives typos, matches as you type and needs no internet."},"892":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#edit","content":"Edit an `.mdx` file, or add one and list it in its folder's `meta.json`. Each page starts with:"},"893":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#edit","content":"`Callout`, `Tabs`, `Steps`, `Cards` and `Accordions` are available on every page without imports."},"894":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#preview-while-you-write","content":"Run both in `notebook/docs`. The site is at `http://localhost:3000/docs` and reloads on every save."},"895":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#build-and-publish","content":"The first writes `notebook/docs/out`; the second copies it to `notebook/docs/site` and stamps it with a digest of the sources."},"896":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#check","content":"It fails when a generated page is stale, or when the shipped site was built from older sources than the ones in the repository."},"897":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#pages-you-do-not-edit","content":"Everything under a persona's `reference/` folder is generated from the docstrings in `exakit_nb` by `python3 tools/nb_docs.py`. To change a command's text, change its docstring and run the tool."},"898":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/docs-site#links","content":"Link to a page by its address, without the `/docs` prefix and without `.mdx`: `/data-scientist/concepts/descriptor`. Link to a command by its page and name: `/data-scientist/development/reference/describe#describe`. A test fails on a link that leads nowhere."},"899":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"page","content":"How it fits together","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits"},"900":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","content":"The helpers, the guided cards and the kit - which part owns what, and the rules that keep them honest."},"901":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"Three layers"},"902":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-helpers","content":"The helpers"},"903":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-guided-view","content":"The guided view"},"904":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#rules-the-tests-enforce","content":"Rules the tests enforce"},"905":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#notebooks","content":"Notebooks"},"906":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"Further reading"},"907":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"Layer"},"908":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"Where"},"909":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"What it owns"},"910":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"The kit"},"911":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"`setup/`, the `exakit` command"},"912":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"The database, connections, loading, connectors, add-ons, the describe service"},"913":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"The helpers"},"914":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"`notebook/package/exakit_nb/` (`import exakit.nb as nb`)"},"915":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"One Python call for each thing a notebook does"},"916":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"The guided view"},"917":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"`notebook/package/exakit_guide/` (`exakit.guide`)"},"918":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"Cards that call the helpers; UI only"},"919":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#three-layers","content":"A notebook is cells that call the helpers. A card is a form that calls the same helpers. Neither talks to the database on its own."},"920":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-helpers","content":"`exakit.nb` exports about 110 names, listed in All commands. The rules they follow:"},"921":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-helpers","content":"**The connection comes from the kit.** `nb.connect()` opens it from the kit's encrypted store. No helper prints or returns a host, a user or a password."},"922":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-helpers","content":"**The kit does the work.** Describing a table is the kit's describe service; loading is `exakit data-load`; connecting a source is `exakit connect`. The helpers call these, they do not reimplement them."},"923":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-helpers","content":"**SQL is shown.** `nb.sql` records the statement, `nb.show_sql()` prints it, and the workflow logs it."},"924":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-helpers","content":"**Reads are live.** `nb.components()`, `nb.addons()`, `nb.connectors()` and the rest ask the kit when called."},"925":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#the-guided-view","content":"`actions.code(id, **values)` is the exact cell text. `actions.run(id, **values)` runs that text in the notebook's namespace and logs it. A screen changes nothing except through `actions.run`, which is why **Code** can always show what a card did."},"926":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#rules-the-tests-enforce","content":"An action's call exists in its notebook's code section, and calls something `exakit.nb` exports."},"927":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#rules-the-tests-enforce","content":"No screen names a dataset, connector, add-on or notebook. Those are read from the kit."},"928":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-28","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#rules-the-tests-enforce","content":"No action shows a secret: a key is bound at run time, never written into the code."},"929":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-29","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#rules-the-tests-enforce","content":"Every exported helper has a page in these docs, and every link in them leads somewhere."},"930":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-30","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#notebooks","content":"Sources are `notebook/notebooks/<persona>/<NN>_<name>.py` in jupytext percent format. The `.ipynb` and the Marimo version are built from them:"},"931":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-31","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"pyexasol, the Python driver that `nb.connect()` opens, with its documentation."},"932":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-32","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"exapump, the command-line client the kit uses for SQL and loading."},"933":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-33","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"Exasol MCP server, the server behind the AI clients, and its repository."},"934":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-34","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"virtual-schemas, behind the `VS_<NAME>` schemas of external sources."},"935":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-35","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"Exasol Personal, the local database the kit deploys."},"936":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits-36","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/how-it-fits#further-reading","content":"Security, how the layers above handle credentials, the network and models."},"937":{"id":"/data-scientist/development","page_id":"/data-scientist/development","type":"page","content":"Development","breadcrumbs":["Doc