t":"Amazon sales"},"1154":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-54","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`ORDER_LINE_ID`"},"1155":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-55","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`ORDER_DATE`"},"1156":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-56","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"none"},"1157":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-57","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`CANCELLED`"},"1158":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-58","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`CATEGORY`"},"1159":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-59","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`SHIP_STATE`"},"1160":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-60","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"Fraud transactions"},"1161":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-61","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`ROW_ID`"},"1162":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-62","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"none"},"1163":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-63","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"none"},"1164":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-64","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`FRAUDULENT`"},"1165":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-65","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`TRANSACTION_TYPE`"},"1166":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-66","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"`LOCATION`"},"1167":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-67","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#slots-which-column-each-step-uses","content":"In code, two slots keep their older names: `dataset.col(\"date\")` is the `time` slot, and `dataset.col(\"label\")` is the `target`."},"1168":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-68","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"The kit never chooses the **target**, the outcome a model predicts. You do. It must be a column of 0 and 1, or true and false. The card reads the target's values and says how common the outcome is. The rule is in `target_verdict` in `notebook/package/exakit_guide/screens/understand.py`:"},"1169":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-69","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"A target that holds values other than 0 and 1 cannot be predicted. The card says the kit can't count or predict it."},"1170":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-70","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"A target that is 1 in under 1% of rows is too rare to learn from reliably."},"1171":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-71","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"A target that is 1 in under 5% of rows is rare but workable, as in fraud."},"1172":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-72","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"Anything else is a good balance to learn from."},"1173":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-73","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"For `amazon_sales`, `CANCELLED` is 1 in 14.2% of rows (18,332 of 128,975, measured). By that rule it sits in the balanced band."},"1174":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-74","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#the-target-is-yours-to-choose","content":"Two 0/1 columns can answer different questions. Pick the one that is the outcome you want to predict."},"1175":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-75","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#draft-and-approved","content":"A column map starts as a **draft**. A draft can be wrong, and nothing downstream uses it. Explore, Ask and Share open only an **approved** dataset. A step given a draft raises `DescriptorNotApproved`, and the message says how to approve it."},"1176":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-76","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#draft-and-approved","content":"A model never approves roles. The kit never chooses the target. You approve."},"1177":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-77","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#draft-a-column-map-for-a-table","content":"To see the kit's proposal for one table, without the review card:"},"1178":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-78","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#draft-a-column-map-for-a-table","content":"A draft is not approved, and no later step reads it. Read the roles and slots, then approve them as described on Approve the column map."},"1179":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map-79","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/column-map#draft-a-column-map-for-a-table","content":"Back to Explore and understand. Next: Approve the column map."},"1180":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","type":"page","content":"Compare groups and trends","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Explore and understand"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups"},"1181":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","content":"Count rows by group and over time with the SQL shown, and read the plain-language notes the kit writes under each result."},"1182":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"Compare groups and trends"},"1183":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#the-worked-example","content":"The worked example"},"1184":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","content":"Comparing groups answers the first question about most tables: how many rows are in each group, and does the outcome differ between groups? Comparing over time answers whether the counts or the outcome rate move. The kit runs both on the approved dataset. It shows the SQL before each result, and writes a plain-language note under it, so a result is not read without its meaning."},"1185":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"`01_explore` runs two kinds of query on the approved dataset. Each query shows its SQL before its result, and a plain-language note under it."},"1186":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"**Compare groups.** The ten largest entities, with the row count of each category and the percentage where the target is 1."},"1187":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"**Over time.** Rows per period, and the percentage where the target is 1. The period is chosen by the span of the data: a day when the span is up to 62 days, a month up to three years, otherwise a year."},"1188":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"`nb.note_groups` and `nb.note_trend` write the notes under each result. A note says what one row of the result is, in the data's own words, and what the overall figure is. It names the highest and lowest groups. It says which groups have too few rows to trust their percentage. It also says that a difference between groups is not a cause."},"1189":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"Groups with fewer than 30 rows are not compared, because one row moves their percentage a lot."},"1190":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#compare-groups-and-trends","content":"A step that needs a column the dataset does not have skips and says why. The Compare step needs a category, an entity and a target. The Over time step needs a time and a target."},"1191":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#the-worked-example","content":"The worked example uses `amazon_sales`. The dataset README records 128,975 order lines and 14.2% cancelled. Measured on the database, the table holds 128,975 rows, and 18,332 of them have `CANCELLED = 1`, which is 14.2%."},"1192":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#the-worked-example","content":"The time span runs from 31 March to 29 June 2022, which is 90 days, so the over-time step groups by month. The first query above returns one row for each category, with its row count."},"1193":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#the-worked-example","content":"Three of the category counts were measured through the semantic model, which counts the prepared view of the table (128,969 rows, not the table's 128,975). They are the order lines in each category: Set 50,281, kurta 49,874 and Western Dress 15,500. See Semantic views. The repository does not record the output of the notebook's group query itself. To see it, run the cell in `01_explore`."},"1194":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#the-worked-example","content":"A difference between groups is a pattern in the data, not a cause. Check a difference against the notes before you act on it."},"1195":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups#the-worked-example","content":"Back to Explore and understand. Previous: Linked tables. Next: Ask questions in plain language."},"1196":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","type":"page","content":"Is my data sent anywhere?","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Explore and understand"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy"},"1197":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","content":"Which steps keep data on this computer, what a model receives when one is set up, and which steps ask first."},"1198":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"Is my data sent anywhere?"},"1199":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","content":"Not by default. The database and the notebooks run on this computer. A step sends data to an AI model only when you set up a model and turn the step on. The steps below say what each one sends."},"1200":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"Not by default. The database and the notebooks run on this computer."},"1201":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"With no model set up, every step runs on this computer. The rules drafter for Ask sends nothing."},"1202":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"With a model set up, Ask sends the approved column map and your question. It never sends rows."},"1203":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"The themes step sends a sample of texts, and it is off until you turn it on. It is in `01_explore`: set `USE_AI_THEMES = True` in the first cell, with a model set up. Your model then sorts the newest texts where the target is 1 and where it is 0 into themes. It makes two model calls."},"1204":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"A table from a live external source asks before any sampled rows go to a model."},"1205":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"The semantic model's AI proposal receives names, types, grains, column notes and a category's most frequent values. It never receives a row, and never a sensitive column. See Semantic views."},"1206":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"Sensitive columns are never sent to a model. See The column map for how the kit marks them."},"1207":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy#is-my-data-sent-anywhere","content":"Back to Explore and understand. Previous: Ask questions in plain language. Next: When it goes wrong."},"1208":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","type":"page","content":"Explore and understand","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Explore and understand"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore"},"1209":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore","content":"Approve the column map that says what each column means, publish the semantic model, check how tables link, compare groups, and ask questions in plain language."},"1210":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore#the-flow","content":"The flow"},"1211":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"Where you do it"},"1212":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"In this stage"},"1213":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"What you have at the end"},"1214":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore","content":"Before you compute anything from data, you need to know what each column means. Is `CUSTOMER_ID` a name or a count? Is `ORDER_DATE` the day of the order or the day of delivery? Which column is the outcome you want to predict? In this stage the kit proposes an answer for every column, and you confirm it. Once you approve the answers, every later step reads them, and so does every AI client that asks the database."},"1215":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#the-flow","content":"Explore has three parts. Each one reads the approved column map."},"1216":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#the-flow","content":"**The column map.** The kit proposes a role for every column. You approve the roles and the slots."},"1217":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#the-flow","content":"**The semantic model.** Approving publishes the schema's model in Exasol Semantic Views. A semantic model is a named layer over the tables. It says which columns are groups, which are numbers to add up, and how tables link. You can also build one model that spans several sources."},"1218":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#the-flow","content":"**Explore and ask.** You compare groups and trends, and ask questions. A question you ask runs only after you approve its SQL."},"1219":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"Guided view"},"1220":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"Notebook code"},"1221":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"Terminal"},"1222":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"**Understand** in `00_quickstart`: the **Column map** card and the **Semantic views** section."},"1223":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"`00_quickstart`, *Approve the column map*: `nb.review_roles`, `role_review.approve(...)`, `nb.show_roles`, `nb.leakage_check`, `nb.column_map_versions`, `nb.data_map`. *Semantic views*: `nb.semantic_model`, `nb.semantic_sql`, `nb.refresh_semantic`, `nb.semantic_models`."},"1224":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"`exakit describe SCHEMA.TABLE` to draft. `--approve` to approve. `--show`, `--versions`. `--schema` for links. `--model`, `--refresh`, `--models` for semantic models."},"1225":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"**Working on** and **Compare groups and see trends** in `01_explore`."},"1226":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"`01_explore`: `nb.sql` with `nb.show_sql()`, `nb.note_groups`, `nb.note_trend`."},"1227":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"None. These run in the notebook."},"1228":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"**Ask** in `02_ask_your_data`."},"1229":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"`02_ask_your_data`: `nb.example_questions`, `nb.draft_question`, `nb.ask`, `proposal.edit(...)`, `proposal.approve()`, `proposal.run()`."},"1230":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"None."},"1231":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#where-you-do-it","content":"The notebooks keep their choices in their first cell. `DATASET = None` follows the dataset you approved last."},"1232":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"The column map\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/column-map\" description=\"What each column is for: roles, slots, the target, and drafts\" />"},"1233":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Approve the column map\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/approve-the-column-map\" description=\"What approval checks and writes, the amazon_sales example, and versions\" />"},"1234":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Semantic views\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views\" description=\"Publish a model, ask it in Semantic SQL, and measured results\" />"},"1235":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Linked tables\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables\" description=\"The schema map: which tables link, and how to correct the links\" />"},"1236":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Compare groups and trends\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/compare-groups\" description=\"Count rows by group and over time, with notes you can read\" />"},"1237":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-28","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Ask questions in plain language\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/questions\" description=\"Draft a read-only query, read it, approve it, then run it\" />"},"1238":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-29","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"Is my data sent anywhere?\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/data-privacy\" description=\"What leaves this computer, and when\" />"},"1239":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-30","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#in-this-stage","content":"<Card title=\"When it goes wrong\" href=\"/data-scientist/explore/troubleshooting\" description=\"Approval, semantic model, and question errors, with the fix\" />"},"1240":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-31","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"An approved column map, saved as a version, and a published semantic model that AI clients can read. You have also seen how the tables link, and you have compared groups and asked questions with queries you approved."},"1241":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore-32","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore#what-you-have-at-the-end","content":"Next: Prepare."},"1242":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","type":"page","content":"Linked tables","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Explore and understand"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables"},"1243":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","content":"How the kit finds the links between the tables of one schema, how to correct them, and how an approved schema map becomes relationships in the semantic model."},"1244":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#the-schema-map-how-tables-link","content":"The schema map: how tables link"},"1245":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#correct-the-map","content":"Correct the map"},"1246":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#where-the-links-go-next","content":"Where the links go next"},"1247":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","content":"Many datasets are several tables. Orders link to customers, and orders link to products. A **schema map** shows how the tables of one schema fit together. It lists every table with its rows and its key, and every link between tables, with the evidence for it. You check the map here, so that the semantic model and the AI clients can follow the links correctly."},"1248":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#the-schema-map-how-tables-link","content":"A link is a column whose values are all in another table's key. The kit finds two kinds:"},"1249":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#the-schema-map-how-tables-link","content":"a foreign key the database declares, or"},"1250":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#the-schema-map-how-tables-link","content":"a column that names another table's key, such as `O_CUSTKEY` and `C_CUSTKEY`, or `CUSTOMER_ID` and `CUSTOMERS.ID`, whose values are all in that key. At most 0.1% of its values may be missing. A name alone never makes a link."},"1251":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#the-schema-map-how-tables-link","content":"The kit's search is in `describe_schema` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/schema_map.py`. Each link it finds shows the evidence beside it."},"1252":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#correct-the-map","content":"Correct the map when the search misses a link or finds a wrong one:"},"1253":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#correct-the-map","content":"Approving the schema map writes its next version. It rebuilds the schema's semantic model with the links as relationships, so AI clients can follow them. The schema's own tables are never changed."},"1254":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#where-the-links-go-next","content":"The links become relationships in the semantic model. See Semantic views."},"1255":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#where-the-links-go-next","content":"The joined view brings linked columns into one wide view of one table. It is built in the Prepare stage: see Linked tables and the joined view."},"1256":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/linked-tables#where-the-links-go-next","content":"Back to Explore and understand. Previous: Semantic views. Next: Compare groups and trends."},"1257":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","type":"page","content":"Ask questions in plain language","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Explore and understand"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions"},"1258":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","content":"Ask a question in English, let the kit draft one read-only SQL query, read it, approve the exact text, and run it through the Exasol MCP server."},"1259":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Questions in plain language"},"1260":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#answers-come-from-the-semantic-model","content":"Answers come from the semantic model"},"1261":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#read-edit-and-approve","content":"Read, edit and approve"},"1262":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#what-the-kit-allows","content":"What the kit allows"},"1263":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#the-worked-example","content":"The worked example"},"1264":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","content":"You can ask a question in English. The kit drafts one read-only SQL query. You read it, approve it, and only then does it run. Nothing runs against your data before you approve the exact text, and the query runs as a read-only user."},"1265":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Ask a question in English. The kit drafts one read-only SQL query. You read it, approve it, and only then does it run."},"1266":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"The three ways differ in who drafts the SQL:"},"1267":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Way"},"1268":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Who drafts"},"1269":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Sent anywhere"},"1270":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"`nb.draft_question(...)`"},"1271":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"The kit's rules. They match common question shapes: a rate, count, total or average per group, a top N, a trend over time, a filter by year or by a group's value."},"1272":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Nothing leaves this computer. No model is called."},"1273":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"`nb.ask(dataset, question)`"},"1274":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Your AI model"},"1275":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"The approved column map and the question. No rows."},"1276":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"`nb.ask(..., proposed_sql=...)`"},"1277":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"You"},"1278":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"Nothing"},"1279":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#questions-in-plain-language","content":"The rules drafter needs no model. `nb.ask` needs one, which you set up with `nb.model_setup()`. Without a model, `nb.ask` stops and says to pass `proposed_sql=` with SQL you wrote. To set up a model, see Set up Exasol."},"1280":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#answers-come-from-the-semantic-model","content":"When a semantic model covers your data, your AI model answers from it. It is shown the model's metrics and dimensions, writes Semantic SQL over the published view, and Exasol Semantic Views compiles that into the plain SQL you read and approve. An AI client over MCP uses the same definitions, so the notebook and the client give the same number for the same question."},"1281":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#answers-come-from-the-semantic-model","content":"Measured: for *revenue by region*, the draft `SELECT region, total_amount FROM SEM_XSRC_TEST.ORDER_LINES GROUP BY region` compiled to a `SUM(AMOUNT)` over `AMAZON.ORDER_LINES` with a `LEFT JOIN` to the state table, the join the model defines. A draft naming a metric the model does not have was refused (`SEMANTIC_QUERY_020`)."},"1282":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#answers-come-from-the-semantic-model","content":"The proposal says which model answered and shows the Semantic SQL under *What the model assumed*. If no model covers the data, or the question cannot be put in the model's terms, the SQL is drafted from the approved column map and the proposal says so under *Check before you approve*."},"1283":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#read-edit-and-approve","content":"Read the proposal, and edit it if it is wrong:"},"1284":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#read-edit-and-approve","content":"Approve it, then run it:"},"1285":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#read-edit-and-approve","content":"In the notebook, set `APPROVE_SQL = True` in the Ask cell and run it again. Nothing runs before approval."},"1286":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-28","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#read-edit-and-approve","content":"Only the exact text you approved runs. `run()` checks the text against the approval. Any change after approval, including an edit, means the query must be approved again."},"1287":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-29","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#read-edit-and-approve","content":"Each approval adds a line to the workflow log with the question, the approved SQL, its digest and the column map version. Each run adds a line with the row count."},"1288":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-30","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#what-the-kit-allows","content":"The kit accepts one `SELECT` or `WITH` statement. It refuses a second statement, and any statement that writes, such as `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `CREATE`, `DROP`, `GRANT` or `COPY`."},"1289":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-31","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#what-the-kit-allows","content":"The query runs through the Exasol MCP server, on the read-only user, and never on the notebook's connection. The server returns at most 1,000 rows. The notebook says so when it returns that many."},"1290":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-32","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#the-worked-example","content":"The worked example is the question in the notebook: `How many rows are in each category?` on `amazon_sales`. For a rate, the rules drafter's own documentation uses the question `Which category has the highest cancellation rate?` on the same table."},"1291":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-33","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#the-worked-example","content":"Read the SQL the kit returns before you approve it. The repository does not record the SQL or the answer for either question. To see the SQL the kit drafts, draft the question and read the proposal's SQL. Drafting is not approval, so nothing runs:"},"1292":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-34","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#the-worked-example","content":"Check the figures against the SQL yourself."},"1293":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions-35","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/questions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/questions#the-worked-example","content":"Back to Explore and understand. Previous: Compare groups and trends. Next: Is my data sent anywhere?."},"1294":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"page","content":"Semantic views","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Explore and understand"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views"},"1295":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","content":"What the published semantic model holds, how to publish one model across tables, how to ask it in Semantic SQL, how the AI proposes fields, and the measured results."},"1296":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"What the model holds"},"1297":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#publish-the-semantic-model","content":"Publish the semantic model"},"1298":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#ask-the-model-in-semantic-sql","content":"Ask the model in Semantic SQL"},"1299":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"Measured on a real database"},"1300":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#one-model-across-sources","content":"One model across sources"},"1301":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#links-across-sources","content":"Links across sources"},"1302":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-a-links-confidence-means","content":"What a link's confidence means"},"1303":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#let-an-ai-or-an-ai-client-propose-links","content":"Let an AI, or an AI client, propose links"},"1304":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-is-stored","content":"What is stored"},"1305":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-it-costs-on-large-data","content":"What it costs on large data"},"1306":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#the-ai-proposes-the-fields","content":"The AI proposes the fields"},"1307":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#keep-the-model-current","content":"Keep the model current"},"1308":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","content":"A **semantic model** is a named layer over your tables. It says which columns are groups, which are numbers to add up, and how tables link. A question can then name a metric, such as the number of order lines, and a group, such as the month, instead of repeating the formula. The kit publishes the model with **Exasol Semantic Views**, a database add-on that stores the model inside the database. Your AI clients read the same model, so the notebook and the clients give the same answer."},"1309":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"The model's structure follows the column maps:"},"1310":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"A table is an **entity**."},"1311":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"A link between tables is a **relationship**, many to one, with its key mapping."},"1312":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-17","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"A time, category or flag column is a **dimension**. A time column also gives a `<column>_month` dimension."},"1313":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-18","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"A measure is a **fact**, with the metrics `total_<column>` and `average_<column>`."},"1314":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-19","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"Every table has a row count, `<entity>_count`."},"1315":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-20","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"A sensitive column is never exposed."},"1316":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-21","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#what-the-model-holds","content":"The approval publishes a model for the schema, named after the schema. For `AMAZON.ORDER_LINES` that model is published as `SEM_AMAZON`. The model is built from the approved column maps of the schema and its approved links."},"1317":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-22","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#publish-the-semantic-model","content":"The quickstart's semantic cell builds a model that can span several tables:"},"1318":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-23","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#publish-the-semantic-model","content":"With `PUBLISH_SEMANTIC = False` the cell shows what it would build and writes nothing. Set it to `True` and run the cell again to publish. The published schema is named after the model in upper case: `SEM_MY_DATA`. `semantic.views` lists the views it holds."},"1319":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-24","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#publish-the-semantic-model","content":"A model can span any number of schemas: sample datasets, your own files and folders, and connected external sources, which are read live. Every table in it needs an approved column map first."},"1320":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-25","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#ask-the-model-in-semantic-sql","content":"Semantic SQL is a query over a semantic view. You name metrics and group them by dimensions. Get the exact names from the model first, then write the query:"},"1321":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-26","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#ask-the-model-in-semantic-sql","content":"Replace the placeholders with names from `semantic.fields`. The metric names follow the pattern `total_<column>` and `average_<column>`, and every table gets a row-count metric. `nb.semantic_sql` switches Semantic SQL on for the session before it runs your query, and it runs the query read-only."},"1322":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-27","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"These results come from a local Exasol Personal database, measured on 12 November 2026. The queries are read-only."},"1323":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-28","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"`exakit marketplace semantic-views` installed Exasol Semantic Views v0.6.1 into the database."},"1324":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-29","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"A model was built over the approved prepared view `EXAKIT_PREPARED.AMAZON__ORDER_LINES` and over `EXAKIT_PREPARED.RETAIL_SIGNALS__CUSTOMER_REVIEWS__PLAIN`, and published. It has 2 views, 20 dimensions and 6 metrics: `amazon_order_lines_count`, `total_qty`, `average_qty`, `total_amount`, `average_amount`, and the reviews' row count."},"1325":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-30","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"The prepared view `EXAKIT_PREPARED.AMAZON__ORDER_LINES` holds 128,969 rows. The raw table `AMAZON.ORDER_LINES` holds 128,975. The view drops 6 order lines that are the same as another line in every column but their row id: see Cleaned views."},"1326":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-31","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"**Counting by month.** The model counts what its view holds:"},"1327":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-32","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"`order_date_month`"},"1328":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-33","page_id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views#measured-on-a-real-database","content":"`amazon_order_lines_count`"},"1329":{"id":"/data-scientist/explore/semantic-views-34","page_id":"/d