nt":"`joined_view(main: Any) -> JoinedView`"},"4170":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/schema-map-54","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/schema-map","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/schema-map#SchemaMap","content":"The proposal: `main` (a dataset, or a table name) with every table its links reach."},"4171":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"page","content":"Semantic","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Data Scientist","Development","Notebook commands"],"tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic"},"4172":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-0","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","content":"Semantic views from a notebook: Exasol Semantic Views models over any of your data."},"4173":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-1","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#find_links","content":"nb.find_links"},"4174":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-2","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#refresh_semantic","content":"nb.refresh_semantic"},"4175":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-3","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#semantic_model","content":"nb.semantic_model"},"4176":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-4","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#semantic_models","content":"nb.semantic_models"},"4177":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-5","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#semantic_sql","content":"nb.semantic_sql"},"4178":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-6","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#SemanticModel","content":"nb.SemanticModel"},"4179":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-7","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","content":"A model spans as many schemas as you give it: sample datasets, your own loaded files and folders, and any number of\nconnected external sources (virtual schemas, read live). Every table needs an approved column map first: its roles\ndecide the model's fields - time, category and flag columns become dimensions, measures become facts with total\\_ and\naverage\\_ metrics, every table gets a row count. The approved links of each schema are used, and `links` adds the\nones across schemas (SCHEMA.TABLE.COLUMN=SCHEMA.TABLE.KEY, the key side unique)."},"4180":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-8","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","content":"With `ai=True` (the default when a model is configured: nb.model_setup) your model proposes business names,\ncomments and synonyms for every field and the metrics people will ask for - from names, types, grains, column notes\nand a category's top values only, never a row and never a sensitive column. Every proposed metric is checked before\nit is offered, and the product validates it again when it is applied: a refused one is skipped and named."},"4181":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-9","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","content":"The work is the kit's describe service (the one `exakit describe --model` runs), loaded from the kit copy. A model\nthe kit builds is marked as such; one of the same name somebody else made is never touched."},"4182":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-10","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#find_links","content":"The links between tables of different schemas - sample data, your files, external sources - where no foreign\nkey says so and the names differ, found from the values, each with its evidence (`exakit describe --links`).\nBounded reads only; nothing is written. `ai` (default: when a model is configured) lets your model propose more\npairs from names, types and notes; `check` is pairs you have in mind (\"S.T.COL=S.T.KEY\"); each is checked against\nthe data and comes back with its evidence or under \"rejected\". Returns \\{links, partial, ...}; a link's\n`confidence` is \"low\" for plain numbers, which match by chance: confirm those before using them."},"4183":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-11","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#refresh_semantic","content":"Rebuild a model the kit published, with the column maps and links approved since (`exakit describe --model\nNAME --refresh`). Approving a column map refreshes every model it is in by itself."},"4184":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-12","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#semantic_model","content":"Build (or with `preview=True` only show) an Exasol Semantic Views model over `tables` - SCHEMA.TABLE of any\nschemas, each with an approved column map - joined along each schema's approved links and `links`\n(SCHEMA.TABLE.COLUMN=SCHEMA.TABLE.KEY). `schemas` takes every table of those schemas that has an approved column\nmap; `find=True` finds the links between the schemas from their values (nb.find_links) and uses the ones the\ndata backs. `ai` (default: when a model is configured) lets your model propose the\nfields' business names, synonyms and extra metrics. Publishing installs Semantic Views when the database does not\nhave it, replaces the model the kit built before under that name, and grants it to the read-only MCP user."},"4185":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-13","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#semantic_models","content":"Every semantic model in the database: name, published schema, whether the kit built it (and can refresh it)."},"4186":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-14","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#semantic_sql","content":"Run Semantic SQL (a query over a published semantic view: its metrics with GROUP BY its dimensions) on this\nconnection: Semantic SQL is switched on for the session first. A read only, shown before its result."},"4187":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-15","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#SemanticModel","content":"A semantic model: what it holds (`spec`), what the AI proposed, and - once published - its views."},"4188":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-16","page_id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic#SemanticModel","content":"Member"},"4189":{"id":"/data-scientist/development/reference/semantic-1