rget - a table with only a key, a text and a 0/1 target has no other group, and counting by that target is its overview."],[3476,"(role, SCHEMA.TABLE.COLUMN) for every role, for a table the notebook prints."],[653,"**`nb.check_model_compatibility(conn, model_version, alias=\"PYTHON3\", model_path=\"model.pkl\")`** checks a pickled scikit-learn model before anything is scored in the database. It refuses when the model's scikit-learn version differs from the version in the database's Python container. The Data Scientist notebooks do not pickle or score models, so this check belongs to the ML Engineer's scoring notebook."],[652,"**`nb.data_checks`** reports repeated keys and ids, exact copies of a row, empty cells, placeholder values such as `Unknown Device`, and a rare label. The repeated keys and copies can inflate a score when a copied row lands on both sides of the split. The data checks are documented on the data checks reference page."],[651,"**`nb.leakage_check`** lists the columns that give the answer away. It tests each column that is not the target, a key, an identifier or free text. The approval runs it too. See Leakage."],[820,"**`nb.note_groups`** is for a per-group result. It says what one row is, the overall figure, the highest and the lowest group, which groups have too few rows to trust (fewer than 30), and that a difference between groups is not a cause."],[822,"**`nb.note_model`** is for the small model's score. It says what the score means in words (the ranges are on Reading ROC AUC), how many rows the model learned from, and the baseline to beat."],[821,"**`nb.note_trend`** is for a per-period result. It names the busiest period and the highest and lowest percentage, leaves out periods with fewer than 30 rows, and says that a change over time shows when something moved, not why."],[367,"**`require` encrypts without checking.** `tls_fix()` says it is used \"only knowingly\". The engine's own advice in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/sql.py` calls it \"encrypts without checking the certificate\"."],[3216,"**1. Find how they join.** No foreign key exists. The kit compares values:"],[3218,"**2. Put one model over them.**"],[3220,"**3. Ask it.** Revenue by region needs both sources. In Semantic SQL you name what you want, not how to join:"],[1714,"**A category column that holds numbers, read as a number**, when it has more than two values. The model can then learn that more is worse, or better."],[615,"**A date in the column map gives a time split.** This is the default. The oldest rows train the model and the newest rows test it."],[1627,"**A folder** loads every CSV, Parquet and JSON file at its top level, one table per file, each named after its file. Subfolders are skipped. The schema is asked once."],[2574,"**A local Exasol database.** It runs in a container. The kit uses Docker when Docker is running, and Podman otherwise. The kit never installs or starts Docker itself."],[1712,"**A measure compared with its category's usual value**, for the first two measures and the first two categories."],[1352,"**A metric that does not exist.** A proposed metric that names a fact the model does not have was refused, and the refusal gave the reason."],[1351,"**A metric the AI proposed.** An AI-style proposal added `cancelled_amount`, the sum of `amount` where `status` is `Cancelled`. Its value is 6,919,284.30. That equals `SELECT SUM(AMOUNT) ... WHERE STATUS = 'Cancelled'` on the table. The proposal also added `cancelled_share`, which is `cancelled_amount / total_amount`. It is 0.088, or 8.8%."],[368,"**A pasted string can change the setting.** `tls_sqlserver()` in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/paste.py` maps `encrypt=false` to `disable`, and `TrustServerCertificate=true` to `require`. Those values come from the string you paste, not from the kit's defaults. Before you confirm the connection, the kit shows the reading back with its secrets masked. The shell prints it as `Read: ...` in `_exakit_vs_tui_paste()` in `setup/lib/virtual-schemas.sh`."],[1830,"**A rare answer.** Under 5% of rows are 1s, so the lab scores with PR AUC, which focuses on finding the rare rows. The lab records the reason in `lab.run.notes`: \"The 1s are rare, so the score is PR AUC, which focuses on finding them.\""],[2161,"**A rare outcome that is not rare.** `CANCELLED` is 1 in 14.2% of rows, measured as 18,332 of 128,975. That is above the 10% line, so the checks do not report a rare outcome."],[2577,"**A read-only AI connection.** The kit's MCP server gives AI clients read access to the database, as a database user that cannot write. Passwords stay in `~/.exasol-starter-kit/credentials/`. Connecting a client is covered in the kit's AI page."],[2492,"**A real model call.** In `02_ask_your_data`, set `DRAFT_WITH_MODEL = True` and run the Ask cells. The model drafts SQL from your question and the column map. Read the SQL, and the model's assumptions and doubts, before you approve anything. A failed call says what went wrong."],[2159,"**A repeated id that is by design.** `ORDER_ID` is an identifier. The descriptor records 120,378 distinct values in 128,975 rows, measured on the database. That is 8,597 repeats. An order has several lines, so repeats are expected. The check still names it, because 93% of the rows are distinct, which is above the 90% line. The line reads:"],[2580,"**Agent skills** for AI clients, placed where CLI agents look (`~/.claude/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`)."],[2458,"**Already running?** The server read its environment when it started. A kernel restart does not read it again. Run `exakit notebook stop`, then `exakit notebook`, in the terminal that has the variables. For Marimo, use `exakit notebook marimo` the same way."],[2355,"**API key:** paste it into the hidden box. **How do I get this?** under the box opens this page at your provider's section; the second link opens the provider's own key page. A server on this computer (Ollama) needs no key."],[2485,"**Approve roles.** Only you approve a column map."],[486,"**Approving a column map.** Approval writes a descriptor under `~/.exasol-starter-kit/descriptors/`. It changes the database only through Semantic Views, in the schemas listed above."],[1228,"**Ask** in `02_ask_your_data`."],[640,"**Behind the charts.** The same filtered rows as a table, sortable, with **Export CSV**."],[888,"**Build:** a static site built with Node on a maintainer's machine. Users never need Node."],[638,"**Category comparison.** A bar chart of the values in the current selection."],[1087,"**Check these first.** The card lists the columns the kit was least sure about, first. `SHIP_CITY` and `SHIP_STATE` are both drafted at low confidence. The kit's evidence for `SHIP_STATE` is 47 distinct values. The card shows that evidence beside it."],[2192,"**Check.** Run the data checks, and read each finding."],[2095,"**Cleaning.** Text is trimmed. A blank cell becomes `NULL`. A placeholder becomes `NULL`, where the placeholder can be listed."],[17,"**Code** turns the same card into its code as cells. Each cell shows the exact `exakit.nb` call behind a control. You can edit and run those cells."],[1186,"**Compare groups.** The ten largest entities, with the row count of each category and the percentage where the target is 1."],[639,"**Composition.** A share-of-total chart. The kit draws it only when you pass `measure=\"count\"`, because a total of a measure that is not a count has no meaning. With more than eight rows shown, the note says smaller categories are grouped as Other."],[2096,"**Copies.** Rows that are exact copies of another row are dropped. The first row, by the key, is kept. When the table has no key, a row that copies another in every column is dropped."],[1338,"**Counting by category.** Grouping by `category` gives the top rows:"],[1326,"**Counting by month.** The model counts what its view holds:"],[2195,"**Create.** Create only the views you want. Each view is described and approved, and the steps after it read the view."],[1625,"**CSV, Parquet and JSON** work. JSON can be an array of records or one record per line. A nested value is kept as JSON text."],[2923,"**Data Scientist.** Four notebooks that take a table from loading to a shared dashboard: agree what each column means, prepare, explore, train a small model, ask questions in plain language and hand the work off. It gets the dashboard server first and then the notebooks, because the notebooks' Share step publishes a dashboard there. See Data Scientist."],[2909,"**Data Scientist** (`data-scientist`)"],[1819,"**different for (almost) every row, so there is nothing general to learn**: a key or an identifier."],[2970,"**Documentation:** dbt's Exasol setup page, and the repository README."],[2994,"**Documentation:** Exasol's user-defined functions, Python in UDFs, and BucketFS."],[2982,"**Documentation:** repository README, and Exasol's JSON functions."],[2966,"**Documentation:** repository README."],[2974,"**Documentation:** repository README."],[2978,"**Documentation:** repository README."],[2986,"**Documentation:** repository README."],[2990,"**Documentation:** repository README. The driver the notebooks use is documented in Exasol's PyExasol page."],[2358,"**Effort:** pick how hard the model thinks, where the model takes an effort setting (see Choose the model and its effort). Otherwise the card says the model uses its own."],[2575,"**exapump**, the command-line tool that loads data files and runs SQL."],[1626,"**Excel** works too. The kit makes a CSV or UTF-8 copy next to your file and says so. Your original is not changed."],[1218,"**Explore and ask.** You compare groups and trends, and ask questions. A question you ask runs only after you approve its SQL."],[776,"**F1** combines how many flagged rows are right and how many real 1s are found, at one cut-off (0.5 here)."],[1460,"**Fill in the details** shows every question below. **Paste a connection string** adds a box for the string: paste it and the kit reads it (`nb.read_connection_string`), fills in the details, and marks only what the string did not hold."],[637,"**Filters.** Categories, a Show setting (10 by default), Sort by, and Chart layout (horizontal or vertical bars). Top N applies after the filters. **Reset filters** clears them."],[1820,"**free text: see the feature ideas**: a text column."],[1541,"**Gaps.** Some values are missing, and the README says about 5% of `DEVICE_USED` and `PAYMENT_METHOD` are NULL."],[2924,"**General.** The kit's own sample data and nothing else: a database to query with SQL or an AI client. Its datasets are bundled with the kit and load without internet."],[2913,"**General** (`general`)"],[1817,"**gives the answer away**: the column is in the list from `nb.leakage_check`. See Leakage on the Evaluate pages."],[16,"**Guided** is the default. You click, choose and approve."],[492,"**Hand-off bundles** get mode 700 for folders and 600 for files, set by `handoff_bundle.py`. Those modes are not applied on Windows, which the code checks with `os.name != \"nt\"`."],[1711,"**How long ago the date was**, when the table has a date. Newer and older records can behave differently."],[2964,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace dash-server`"],[2968,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace dbt-exasol`"],[2972,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace exasol-scheduler`"],[2976,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace exasol-vscode`. If you installed the extension yourself, the kit leaves it as yours and does not replace it."],[2980,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace json-tables`"],[2988,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace notebook`. It offers two front ends, JupyterLab and Marimo. See Notebooks."],[2984,"**Install:** `exakit marketplace semantic-views`. The first approval of a column map installs it into the database if it is missing, as Semantic Views describes."],[2992,"**Install:** Not available in this release. The catalog marks the add-on `visible: false`, and `exakit marketplace udf-devkit` refuses it with \"is disabled in marketplace/catalog.json\"."],[2199,"**Is the data clean?** in the **Explore and predict** card, with **Linked tables** and **A cleaned copy** under it. Each has a button that creates the view."],[2892,"**It appears where `order` puts it** when `visible` and `picker` are true."],[1951,"**It does not alert.** Tracing and the log record what happened. They do not send a message."],[1948,"**It does not detect drift.** It does not compare this month's data with last month's, and it does not warn you when a score falls."],[1949,"**It does not retrain.** A model is trained when you run its cell, and nothing runs it again on a schedule."],[1950,"**It does not watch the database.** Nothing checks the table between your runs."],[2894,"**Its documentation** goes in `notebook/docs/content/docs/<id>/`. See These docs."],[2893,"**Its notebooks** go in `notebook/notebooks/<id>/`, listed in `notebook/catalog.json`."],[2094,"**Kept as text.** An identifier or key column stays text. So does a number with a leading zero, and a column named like a code, such as a postal code, a phone number, an SKU, or a name ending in `_ID`."],[750,"**Leave it out with `leaks=`.** For example: `leaks = nb.leakage_check(connection, dataset) + [\"AMOUNT\"]`, then pass `leaks=leaks` to `nb.model_lab`. The model lab page has the full example."],[2460,"**Leave out what you do not use.** No model ID means the AI steps skip and say so. No Langfuse variables means tracing is off."],[481,"**Loading a file (`exakit data-load`, `nb.load_own_file()`).** The kit does not overwrite a table it did not load. `load_own_file()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/own_file.py` stops with \"already exists and was not loaded by load_own_file\" unless you pass `replace=True`. For `exakit data-load`, `EXAKIT_ON_EXISTING` decides:"],[491,"**Logs and records** are written at mode 600 on Unix. The workflow log is opened with `os.open(..., 0o600)` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/workflow.py`. The virtual schema log is \"created owner-only (mode 600)\" according to `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/vslog.py`."],[749,"**Look at `lab.importance` after every run.** If a column you suspect is at the top, it is a lead to read."],[2193,"**Look at the links.** Read the schema map to see which tables link to yours."],[778,"**MAE** is the average size of the miss, in the number's own units. **RMSE** is similar, but it punishes large misses more."],[1535,"**Many lines per order.** 128,975 lines belong to 120,378 orders, so an order can have more than one line. Count orders with `COUNT(DISTINCT ORDER_ID)` when you mean orders."],[1821,"**marked Leave out**: the column's role is `other`."],[2925,"**ML Engineer.** In-database training and scoring. It also starts a local MLflow server and installs the UDF runtime."],[2917,"**ML Engineer** (`ml-engineer`)"],[2357,"**Model:** pick one. The pencil lets you type any model name instead."],[1363,"**Models in the database** lists every model. **Refresh it** rebuilds a model the kit built, from the column maps and links approved since."],[616,"**No date means no time split.** The kit refuses a time split that the data cannot support, and names the fix. A dataset with no date, such as `fraud_transactions`, needs a split named on purpose: `nb.write_handoff(conn, ds, split=\"random\")`."],[1831,"**No date.** The table has only the hour of the day. The lab splits at random, stratified so both sides keep the same share of fraud. With no date it sorts by the first column of its query and keeps 50,000 of the 51,000 rows. The notebook baseline reads the first 20,000 rows by `ROW_ID` and leaves the month out."],[2891,"**No secrets and no machine paths.** A persona names things, it never holds them."],[1187,"**Over time.** Rows per period, and the percentage where the target is 1. The period is chosen by the span of the data: a day when the span is up to 62 days, a month up to three years, otherwise a year."],[1088,"**Personal data.** `SHIP_POSTAL_CODE` is marked sensitive. It is never charted, grouped or sent to a model."],[1818,"**personal data**: the column is marked sensitive in the column map. It is never used as an input."],[2579,"**Personas and sample data.** See the persona section above."],[1832,"**Placeholder values.** The README lists 1,530 `Unknown Device` and 1,530 `Invalid Method` values. The lab does not clean them. Each placeholder becomes one more category value. To read the typed, cleaned view from Prepare instead, set `USE_PREPARED = True` in `01_explore`."],[1540,"**Placeholders.** `DEVICE_USED` has 1,530 rows `Unknown Device`, and `PAYMENT_METHOD` has 1,530 rows `Invalid Method`. They look like categories, but they mean \"not known\"."],[775,"**PR AUC** is the average precision score: how well the model's top-ranked rows contain the rare 1s. It is the one to read when 1s are rare."],[2194,"**Propose a view.** A joined view brings in the linked tables. A typed view fixes types and cleans values. Read the proposal. It shows each change with its evidence and the exact SQL."],[2354,"**Provider:** pick yours from the menu. Its address fills in by itself. To use another address (a proxy, a server on another port), click the pencil beside it and type it."],[2576,"**pyexasol**, the Python driver the notebooks use."],[777,"**R squared** is the share of the spread in the number that the model explains. A value of 0 means no better than the average."],[1353,"**Reading from an AI client.** The read-only MCP user, the one AI clients use, read the model's metrics from `SEMANTIC_CATALOG.METRICS`."],[924,"**Reads are live.** `nb.components()`, `nb.addons()`, `nb.connectors()` and the rest ask the kit when called."],[966,"**Reads are live.** `nb.components()`, `nb.addons()`, `nb.connectors()` and the rest ask the kit when they are called."],[1354,"**Rebuilding on approval.** Approving a column map rebuilt its schema's model. It also refreshed every multi-source model that the table is in."],[1539,"**Repeated ids.** `TRANSACTION_ID` is not unique. 51,000 rows hold 50,000 distinct ids, so 1,000 ids appear twice. The README counts 881 exact copies and 119 copies that disagree. `ROW_ID` is the key for this table."],[2965,"**Repository:** exasol-labs/dash-server"],[2981,"**Repository:** exasol-labs/exasol-json-tables"],[2973,"**Repository:** exasol-labs/exasol-scheduler"],[2985,"**Repository:** exasol-labs/exasol-semantic-views"],[2977,"**Repository:** exasol-labs/exasol-vscode"],[2969,"**Repository:** exasol/dbt-exasol"],[2989,"**Repository:** exasol/notebook-connector"],[2993,"**Repository:** exasol/script-languages-release. This is the upstream repository the kit builds its containers from."],[774,"**ROC AUC** is the chance that the model gives a row with a 1 a higher score than a row with a 0. It is explained on Reading ROC AUC."],[2486,"**Run SQL.** A drafted query runs only after you approve that exact text. Editing the text removes the approval."],[1628,"**Running it again is safe.** A file already loaded is not loaded twice. A file that changed is loaded again. The table keeps the file's SHA-256 in its comment, so the kit can tell."],[490,"**Secrets** are in `credentials/`. See Credentials."],[1504,"**Semantic views** starts with the schemas of your tables ticked, so **Find links between them** and **Publish the model** work on the same choice. See Semantic views."],[890,"**Served:** `nb.docs()` serves that folder from the kit's own Python, on this computer only."],[889,"**Shipped:** the built files, in `notebook/docs/site/`, travel with the kit."],[779,"**Silhouette score** is how close each row sits to its own group, compared with the nearest other group."],[664,"**Small groups.** Groups with fewer than 30 rows are not compared. This applies to the group and trend notes. See The readout notes."],[887,"**Source:** MDX pages in `notebook/docs/content/docs/`, one folder per persona. The folder layout is the sidebar; each folder's `meta.json` sets its title and page order, and a persona's folder is marked `\"root\": true`, which makes it an entry in the list at the top of the sidebar."],[965,"**SQL is shown.** `nb.sql` records each statement, `nb.show_sql()` prints the last one, and the workflow logs it."],[923,"**SQL is shown.** `nb.sql` records the statement, `nb.show_sql()` prints it, and the workflow logs it."],[1536,"**STATUS gives the target away.** Every cancelled line has `STATUS = 'Cancelled'`, and no other line does. Run `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM AMAZON.ORDER_LINES WHERE STATUS = 'Cancelled'`: it returns 18,332, the same as `CANCELLED = 1`. A model that sees `STATUS` learns the answer from it, not from the order. Prepare shows how the kit catches this."],[1469,"**Test connection** checks the inputs and whether this computer reaches the source. It changes nothing and needs no password. **Connect and use this data** runs the kit's connect command with the password on its standard input, then loads what you ticked."],[2162,"**The answer-giving column.** The checks above do not catch `STATUS`. It is found by the leakage check. See Columns that give the answer away."],[1089,"**The answer.** `STATUS` is a category with 13 values, and it gives the answer away: a cancelled order has a cancelled status. The approval warns about it, and the model leaves it out. The leakage check explains how."],[743,"**The approval warns.** When you approve the column map, the card says `STATUS` gives the answer away, because it is filled in after the outcome, and that the model step leaves it out."],[744,"**The baseline never reads it.** The notebook baseline skips a flagged category or entity. On `amazon_sales` it reads only the category, the state and the month."],[1754,"**The baseline** uses three inputs only: the category, the state and the month of `ORDER_DATE`. It does not read `STATUS`, which gives the target away. See Start with a baseline."],[1216,"**The column map.** The kit proposes a role for every column. You approve the roles and the slots."],[921,"**The connection comes from the kit.** `nb.connect()` opens it from the kit's encrypted store. No helper prints or returns a host, a user or a password."],[963,"**The connection comes from the kit.** `nb.connect()` opens it from the kit's encrypted store. No helper prints or returns a host, a user or a password."],[1964,"**The data no longer matches.** With a connection, the check compares the data digest. A mismatch means the rows or the dates differ."],[366,"**The default is `verify`.** `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/paste.py` says \"tls stays at verify (TCPS, the kit's default)\". The engine's failure advice in `setup/virtual-schemas/engine/lifecycle.py`, `tls_fix()`, names the fix: \"fix the server certificate\", or give the CA that signs it with `--set ca=<PEM file or bundle>`."],[1963,"**The descriptor's version changed**, or its digest did. The line gives both versions, such as v2 to v3."],[2399,"**The effort.** A reasoning model can think longer before it answers. The kit sends your choice as the provider's own `reasoning_effort` setting:"],[663,"**The feature rule.** A verdict of `helps` needs a rise of 0.005 in cross-validation and no fall of more than 0.002 on the held-out rows. That is a rule of thumb. See Feature ideas."],[1962,"**The hand-off is for another descriptor.** The line names both ids."],[665,"**The held-out size.** The warnings for a small held-out set are on Hold-out."],[1825,"**The inputs.** Every measure, category and flag column the column map gives a role to, plus the month of the date. Keys, identifiers, text, personal data and the answer-givers are left out."],[369,"**The kit does not lower the setting by itself.** When the paste does not say otherwise, the engine keeps `verify`. An unknown `encrypt` value is reported, and `tls` stays at its default."],[922,"**The kit does the work.** Describing a table is the kit's describe service; loading is `exakit data-load`; connecting a source is `exakit connect`. The helpers call these, they do not reimplement them."],[964,"**The kit does the work.** Describing a table is the kit's describe service. Loading is `exakit data-load`. Connecting a source is `exakit connect`."],[745,"**The lab leaves it out.** `lab_data.left_out` lists `STATUS` with the reason \"gives the answer away\"."],[1694,"**The lab's baseline** is the model called `baseline` in each prediction task. For yes or no, it always gives the common answer. A constant prediction scores 0.5 ROC AUC, so any real model should score above 0.5."],[1755,"**The lab** scores with ROC AUC, because the rarer answer is above 5%. It splits by time, so it learns from the older rows and is tested on the newer ones. See The model lab."],[712,"**The leakage trap.** `STATUS` is `Cancelled` on exactly the 18,332 rows whose `CANCELLED` is 1. A model that reads `STATUS` learns the answer it was given. See Leakage."],[1822,"**The metric.** The rarer answer is 14.2% of rows, which is above 5%, so the lab scores with ROC AUC. Below 5%, it uses PR AUC instead. Both are explained on Reading ROC AUC and Metrics."],[662,"**The model rule.** The lab picks the simpler model when two are within 0.01 of each other. That is a rule for choosing, not a test that the gap is real. See The models the lab tries."],[2398,"**The model.** A larger model drafts better questions and feature ideas, and costs more per call. A small or local model is fine to try the steps. The kit sends the model the column names, types, roles and notes for the step, never a row of your data (see What the kit sends to a model)."],[2578,"**The notebook add-on.** It holds the notebooks' Python, JupyterLab, Marimo and the kernel named **Exasol (Starter Kit)**. The Data Scientist persona installs it."],[1693,"**The notebook baseline** is a small logistic regression in `01_explore`. It uses three inputs: the category, the state and the month of the date."],[2459,"**The plain values can live in the settings file.** `~/.exasol-starter-kit/exakit.env` takes the model's ID and address (`EXAKIT_MODEL_ID`, `EXAKIT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL`, and optionally `EXAKIT_PROVIDER_HEADERS` and `EXAKIT_MODEL_TEMPERATURE`). Remove the `# ` in front of a line and set it once. The file refuses any key, secret or token. It does not hold the Langfuse address or the Langfuse keys. Those stay in the terminal as shown above."],[713,"**The rare answer.** `fraud_transactions` has 2,510 fraud rows out of 51,000 (4.9%). A model that always says \"not fraud\" is about 95% accurate, so accuracy is the wrong score. See Rare answers."],[1823,"**The rows.** `amazon_sales` has 128,975 rows, so the lab reads the newest 50,000 by `ORDER_DATE`."],[1217,"**The semantic model.** Approving publishes the schema's model in Exasol Semantic Views. A semantic model is a named layer over the tables. It says which columns are groups, which are numbers to add up, and how tables link. You can also build one model that spans several sources."],[1824,"**The split.** `ORDER_DATE` is a date, so the lab uses a time split. It learns from the oldest 75% of those rows and is tested on the newest 25%."],[661,"**The spread across folds.** `cv_sd` on the board shows how much a model's score moves between folds. A gap between two models that is smaller than that spread is not a reliable difference."],[2494,"**The trace.** Open your Langfuse project and its traces. Each model call from the kit is one trace, with its input, its output and, when the provider returns them, its token counts. Themes in Explore make two calls, and both go into one trace named `explore-themes`."],[1713,"**Two categories together**, such as a category with a certain state."],[2093,"**Types.** A text column whose every value is a number becomes `DECIMAL`. A column with exponents becomes `DOUBLE`. A text column whose every value is a date becomes `DATE`, in one of these formats: `YYYY-MM-DD`, `DD-MM-YYYY`, `DD.MM.YYYY`, `MM/DD/YYYY`, or `DD/MM/YYYY`. A timestamp becomes `TIMESTAMP`, and true or false becomes `BOOLEAN`."],[1222,"**Under