what each column of a table is for. Its technical name is the **descriptor**, the word you see in code and in some file names. Every later query is built from the column map, so no notebook types your column names. A notebook asks for \"the time column\" or \"the target\", and the column map gives the name."],[1128,"A **confidence** says how close the evidence is to the line the rule draws. It is not a probability that the business meaning is right. Low-confidence columns come first in the review. For `amazon_sales`, the card lists `SHIP_CITY` and `SHIP_STATE` first, both drafted at low confidence. The card shows the evidence beside each column. For `SHIP_STATE` that evidence is 47 distinct values."],[1573,"A **dataset** is a table with its approved column map. The column map says what each column is for: its role, and the slot the steps use. The notebooks never read a table directly. They read a dataset and build every query from its roles."],[2170,"A **feature** is a new input for a model, built from the columns you have. For example, \"how long ago the order date was\" is a feature built from one column. The kit proposes feature ideas from your data, and it tests each one before you keep it. The model is trained in the Model stage. These helpers belong to the model lab, and you can call them from here."],[2239,"A **joined view** is a view that combines your table with the tables it links to. Each row of your table keeps one row, and the linked columns are added beside it. You need one when the questions you ask use columns that live in another table. The links come from the schema map, which you check in Linked tables in the Explore stage."],[2903,"A **persona** is a named set of answers to the questions the installer asks: which datasets to load, which AI clients to connect and which add-ons to install. Choosing a persona is choosing what this computer is for."],[39,"A **persona** is the kit set up for one kind of work. It decides which datasets are loaded, which add-ons are installed and which notebooks you get. See What is a persona."],[1308,"A **semantic model** is a named layer over your tables. It says which columns are groups, which are numbers to add up, and how tables link. A question can then name a metric, such as the number of order lines, and a group, such as the month, instead of repeating the formula. The kit publishes the model with **Exasol Semantic Views**, a database add-on that stores the model inside the database. Your AI clients read the same model, so the notebook and the clients give the same answer."],[3007,"A **skill** is a set of instructions that teaches an AI client how to use the kit: which command loads data, how to read a column map, where dashboards go."],[1129,"A **slot** names the one column a step reads."],[2108,"A **target** is the column you want to predict. The **label** in the code is the same column. ROC AUC is a score from 0 to 1 for how well a model puts the rows with a 1 above the rows with a 0. A model that guesses at random scores 0.5."],[2643,"A **workflow** is one id that every query and decision in a piece of work carries. The quickstart starts it, and every later notebook continues it, so you never paste an id."],[3273,"A `measure` column"],[3271,"A `time`, `category` or `flag` column"],[2407,"A balance"],[1764,"A baseline score, the lab's leaderboard with the model it picked, the importance list, and the feature ideas you kept with their verdicts. The next step is to check the score on rows the model never trained on, in Evaluate."],[3071,"A button or form on a notebook card ran, with the code it ran and whether it worked"],[1604,"A certificate cannot be verified"],[2513,"A check tells you whether the database answers. Run the terminal check once after the install. The quickstart runs the cell check first."],[2210,"A checked table, with the findings you have decided on. Optionally, a joined view and a typed view in `EXAKIT_PREPARED`, each with its own approved column map. The steps after this one read the view you created, or your table if you created none."],[256,"A child process's environment is readable by its owner and by root, not by other users. The kit's comments rely on that. Each variable is unset again after the call that needs it."],[1459,"A choice decides what the next lists offer. The card asks the kit again after each pick (`nb.connector_inputs(source, catalog=...)`) and takes the kit's default for the settings that follow, so the details below are always the ones that combination needs. A choice the kit does not support yet is shown greyed out with its reason."],[555,"A colleague follows the README:"],[1144,"A column fills one slot at most. A slot can be empty when no column fits. An empty slot says why."],[2281,"A column has blank values. An average leaves those rows out, and a grouping puts them in a group of their own. Decide whether a blank means zero, not known, or not applicable. The typed view turns a blank into `NULL`."],[1986,"A column map is drafted, or approved as a new version"],[1175,"A column map starts as a **draft**. A draft can be wrong, and nothing downstream uses it. Explore, Ask and Share open only an **approved** dataset. A step given a draft raises `DescriptorNotApproved`, and the message says how to approve it."],[3059,"A column map was drafted or approved"],[3277,"A column marked **sensitive** is never a dimension and never a public fact."],[2483,"A column marked sensitive is named with its role only, and the model is told never to select its values. A sensitive column is never grouped, charted or sent to a model as data."],[1250,"a column that names another table's key, such as `O_CUSTKEY` and `C_CUSTKEY`, or `CUSTOMER_ID` and `CUSTOMERS.ID`, whose values are all in that key. At most 0.1% of its values may be missing. A name alone never makes a link."],[2217,"A column whose values almost decide the target on their own makes a model look better than it is. Such a column is usually written after the outcome. A cancelled order gets a cancelled status, so the status is known only once the outcome is known. A model that reads it learns the answer, not a pattern, and scores well on the data it was trained on. For an order whose outcome is not yet known, the same column cannot help."],[3091,"A column whose values look like e-mail addresses, phone numbers, IBANs or postcodes is marked **sensitive**."],[2114,"A column with the role `key` or `identifier` where at least 90% of rows are distinct. A value that many rows share, such as a customer on every purchase, is not reported."],[2521,"A command run from a cell has no terminal. A question it would ask takes its default. Give the answer up front instead, for example `nb.exakit(\"mcp-setup\", env={\"EXAKIT_MCP_CLIENTS\": \"claude\"})`."],[3549,"A command's page is found in the site's own list (`commands.json`, written from `exakit.nb`'s `__all__` when the\nsite is built), so every public helper has one. Anything else is handed to the site's search."],[2648,"A connection string with a password is refused too."],[3891,"A connector with several kinds of source in one (Lakehouse: Iceberg REST, Unity, Glue or a plain folder, on S3 or\nAzure) has settings that decide what it asks. `tree` is those settings as the connect menu's tree (a catalog,\nand under it the files it can read), each node with the settings its pick gives; give them here\n(`connector_inputs(\"lakehouse\", catalog=\"unity\", files=\"s3\")`) and `inputs` are that combination's.\n`settings` are the ones in force (given, or the kit's default) and `setting_inputs` the setting questions\nthemselves, each with its `choices`, their `choice_labels` and the default in force. Every other connector\nhas an empty `tree` and no `setting_inputs`."],[2115,"A count of rows counts the repeats twice. Count distinct values, or keep one row per value."],[1994,"A dashboard is previewed or published"],[3799,"A dashboard prepared from an approved dataset and one query."],[4238,"