low log."],[1968,"Back to Monitor and iterate. Next: Tracing model calls."],[2008,"Back to Monitor and iterate. Next: Versions and semantic models."],[2289,"Back to Prepare."],[2163,"Back to Prepare. Next: Columns that give the answer away."],[2274,"Back to Prepare. Previous: A typed, cleaned view. Next: Features."],[2247,"Back to Prepare. Previous: Columns that give the answer away. Next: A typed, cleaned view."],[2232,"Back to Prepare. Previous: Data checks. Next: Linked tables and the joined view."],[2101,"Back to Prepare. Previous: Linked tables and the joined view. Next: Prepared views."],[2183,"Back to Prepare. Previous: Prepared views. Next: When it goes wrong."],[645,"Back to Preview, check, publish. Next: The hand-off bundle."],[2636,"Back to Set up Exasol. Next stage: Get the data."],[2602,"Back to Set up Exasol. Next: Check the kit."],[2583,"Back to Set up Exasol. Next: Open the notebooks."],[2652,"Back to Set up Exasol. Next: Set up AI and Langfuse."],[2506,"Back to Set up Exasol. Next: Troubleshooting."],[2522,"Back to Set up Exasol. Next: Workflows."],[2434,"Base URL"],[1885,"baseline (`baseline`), linear (`linear`), ridge (`ridge`), lasso (`lasso`), decision tree, random forest, extra trees, gradient boosting, k-nearest neighbours"],[1882,"baseline (`baseline`), logistic regression (`logistic`), logistic regression with L1 (`logistic_l1`), decision tree (`decision_tree`), random forest (`random_forest`), extra trees (`extra_trees`), gradient boosting (`boosting`), k-nearest neighbours (`knn`), naive Bayes (`naive_bayes`), linear support vector machine (`svm`)"],[1947,"Be clear about these limits before you rely on the kit for a recurring job."],[1577,"Because queries come from roles, the same cell runs on `amazon_sales`, on `fraud_transactions` and on your own table. A role no column fits is never guessed. `dataset.has(...)` is false, the step that needs it skips, and the step says why."],[511,"Before anything is sent"],[3356,"Before sampled rows go to a model endpoint: True at once for a table in the database; for a live source (the\ndescriptor names a source) it names the source and asks, and only a yes sends. `answer` answers for a script;\nwith no one to ask (a headless run) the answer is no."],[3629,"Before sampled rows go to a model endpoint: True at once for a table in the database; for a live source (the descriptor names a source) it names the source and asks, and only a yes sends."],[1214,"Before you compute anything from data, you need to know what each column means. Is `CUSTOMER_ID` a name or a count? Is `ORDER_DATE` the day of the order or the day of delivery? Which column is the outcome you want to predict? In this stage the kit proposes an answer for every column, and you confirm it. Once you approve the answers, every later step reads them, and so does every AI client that asks the database."],[2190,"Before you compute from data, you check that the data says what you think it says. A repeated ID counts a customer twice. An empty cell drops out of an average without a warning. A placeholder such as `Unknown Device` looks like a category, and it means \"not known\". A column written after the outcome makes a model look perfect. In this stage the kit finds these problems and explains what each one does to a result. It never changes your table. When you ask for a cleaned or joined version, it creates a view next to your table, and that view gets its own approved column map."],[588,"Before you preview: Dash Server"],[840,"Below 0.6"],[2120,"Blank values in any column"],[3384,"Both are built from the descriptor alone - the key, the label and every column's role - so no column name is typed in\na notebook and the same cell runs on every dataset. Each check is one or two SQL statements, run through nb.sql (so\nthey are shown and logged like every other query), and each finding is one plain sentence saying what it does to a\nresult. Nothing is changed in the data: deciding what to do about a finding is the