ook package: each answer is the kit's own - an `exakit ... --json`\ncommand, or a file of the kit copy these notebooks run with (data/datasets/\\*/dataset.conf, notebook/catalog.json,\npersonas/\\*.json, mcp/adapters). The guided view's cards read the same functions, so a card and a cell agree."],[3334,"Nothing here prints a host, a user or a password; an MLflow address is shown without any credentials it holds."],[4048,"Nothing here prints a host, a user or a password."],[1858,"Nothing in the Data Scientist notebooks saves a fitted model to disk or to the database. The lab's fitted model lives in your kernel until you stop it. To keep a result, keep the numbers: the board, the importance list and the notes."],[425,"Nothing is filtered. See the warning below"],[2830,"Nothing is loaded or installed without that screen. Without a terminal and without `EXAKIT_PERSONA`, the kit installs on its own with the General samples: TPC-H, energy and weather."],[2403,"Nothing is sent: the provider decides"],[4121,"Nothing is written before create(): the proposal shows every join and the exact SQL. Then the schema map is approved\n(its links become relationships of the schema's Exasol Semantic Views model, for agents), the view goes into EXAKIT_PREPARED and gets\nits own approved column map - the main table's roles carried over - so every later step reads the joined data."],[3995,"Nothing is written until `create()`: the proposal shows each change with the evidence for it and the exact SQL. Then\nthe view goes into the EXAKIT_PREPARED schema, and it gets its own approved descriptor - the same roles as the table it\ncame from, a column that became a date filling an empty time slot - so every later step reads the prepared data."],[1272,"Nothing leaves this computer. No model is called."],[3208,"Nothing moves"],[2060,"Nothing on its own. Approval checks the data again, and a training hand-off is refused when its data digest no longer matches."],[2064,"Nothing, until the model is rebuilt"],[1785,"Nothing: no outcome is needed"],[1788,"Nothing: no outcome is needed"],[2068,"Nothing. The fitted model is not saved, so each run trains it again."],[2072,"Nothing. The preview was made from the old query."],[2091,"Numbers and dates often arrive as text, when a file loads as text. Blank cells, placeholders and copies make a result quietly wrong. The **typed view** fixes these. It is a view, a stored query that reads your table, so your table is never changed. The kit proposes the changes first, and creates the view only when you call `create`."],[1380,"Numbers get the low mark for a measured reason. A key that is simply 1, 2, 3 and so on holds every small number. On a database with six schemas, the first version of this search proposed 18 links into one such key: a meter id, a quantity and a count of delayed shipments all \"matched\" an order line id. So a number column is only considered when it spans at least half the key's range and something else supports it: a similar name, an approved column map that calls it a key or an identifier, or a pair proposed by you or an AI. Even then the data can only contradict such a link, never prove it. Two were still proposed between unrelated order numbers, which is why the last word is yours."],[3181,"Object not found"],[2421,"Ollama"],[2299,"Ollama (on this computer)"],[710,"On `amazon_sales`, the notebook baseline is scored on a quarter of the rows it did not train on, with ROC AUC. The notebook prints the score. This repository does not record a value for it, so these pages quote none."],[851,"On `amazon_sales`, the rarer answer is 14.2% of rows, so the metric is ROC AUC. On `fraud_transactions`, the rarer answer is 4.9%, so the metric is PR AUC instead. See Rare answers."],[862,"On `fraud_transactions`, 1,000 ids appear twice. A copy on both sides of the split can make the held-out score look better than it is. Run `nb.data_checks` and read the repeated-id finding. See Rare answers."],[2518,"On a fresh machin