rt (so picking fraud_transactions there carries on here), else amazon_sales."],[1017,"The dataset approved last, which the notebooks follow"],[1906,"The dataset has no target, or no category or entity that is free of the answer, so the baseline cannot run. The step says so. Choose a target in the column map, approve it, and run the notebook again. `nb.model_settings()` shows whether the AI steps have a model. No model is needed for the baseline or the lab."],[1415,"The dataset has no text column. `amazon_sales` and `fraud_transactions` have none, so the text steps skip on them and say so."],[2244,"The dataset loads one table into the `AMAZON` schema. The schema map finds no link to another table, so there is no joined view to build:"],[2229,"The dataset README also names `COURIER_STATUS`, `QTY` and `AMOUNT` as changing once an order is cancelled. The check reports the columns it finds from the data, not the ones the README names. So read the printed list rather than assume it."],[463,"The default schema of the read-only user, where local uploads land. Created if it is missing"],[679,"The default when the column map has a date"],[682,"The default when there is no date"],[248,"The definition names the file, `EXA_PASSWORD` with a `valueFile`. The launcher reads the file"],[3335,"The deployment's bucket directory exists on this machine where the kit says it is, and - given a connection -\na UDF sees it at its /buckets path too (skipped, with a note, when the container check fails)."],[3611,"The deployment's bucket directory exists on this machine where the kit says it is, and - given a connection - a UDF sees it at its /buckets path too (skipped, with a note, when the container check fails)."],[3520,"The descriptor document (a copy)."],[3977,"The descriptor is that mapping of role to column, a descriptor mapping (`schema_version`, `id`, `fact_table`,\n`roles`), or a descriptor file - YAML or JSON; `data/datasets/<id>/descriptor.yaml` in the kit shows the shape -\nwhose fact_table then names the table (`table` may be None). Given as a mapping of roles, they become a\nschema_version 2 \\*\\*draft\\*\\* (version 1, drafted by the user, owned by EXAKIT_DESCRIPTOR_OWNER or the OS user, its\n`source` the file) and are written beside the file as `<name>.descriptor.yaml` (when no descriptor is there yet),\nso `DATASET = \"<the file>\"` in a notebook opens it again with that one assignment; approving it (the quickstart's\napprove(), or exakit describe \\<id> --approve) is what the training and question steps wait for, exploring does not.\nThe rows travel over pyexasol's import on the open connection: no\nhost, user or password here, as everywhere in exakit.nb. Column types come from the file; the date role's column\nbecomes a DATE and a true/false column 0 or 1; a JSON file is an array of records or one record per line, its nested\nvalues kept as JSON text. The table is marked as loaded from the file (its comment holds the file's SHA-256), so\nrunning the cell again loads nothing when the file is unchanged and reloads it when it changed; a table of the same\nname that this did not create is refused unless `replace=True`."],[3446,"The descriptor's `source` as written: a dataset id, `file:<path>`, `vs:<alias>` (or a schema_version 1 file's bare alias), or None."],[3801,"The descriptor's roles, one per line, as the notebook will use them."],[4055,"The descriptor's roles, one per line, as the notebook will use them."],[3448,"The descriptor's version: 1 for a first draft or a shipped file; approving writes the next."],[3456,"The digest of the table the descriptor was approved on (its `digest` key, design 2.3: sha256 over the table's name, its columns and types, the row count and an order-independent content hash of the role columns), None until approve computes it."],[3548,"The documentation is a site of plain files the kit ships (`notebook/docs/site` in the kit copy): pages, styles and\na search index, built ahead of time. This module serves that folder from this Python on this computer only\n(the loopback address, a free port) and shows it in a frame under the cell, so reading and searching need no internet, no Node\nand no second install. The server starts on the first call and stops with the kernel."],[995,"The documentation is MDX pages in `notebook/docs/content/docs/`, one folder per persona. The folder layout is the sidebar, and each folder's `meta.json` sets its title and page order."],[3022,"The documentation, from a notebook"],[3090,"The draft is deterministic: the same table gives the same draft."],[3679,"The effort levels this model takes (sent as reasoning_effort); \\[] when the provider documents none for it."],[4023,"The effort levels this model takes (sent as reasoning_effort); \\[] when the provider documents none for it."],[7,"The end-to-end workflow most data scientists follow, in eight stages, and how the Starter Kit supports each one."],[626,"The error reads \"choose another APP_NAME\". The name is taken by a dashboard the kit did not make, or one made for another dataset. Choose another `APP_NAME`. The kit never overwrites a dashboard that belongs to a different dataset."],[1910,"The error reads \"No model could be trained on this data\", and lists the reason for each model that failed. The most common cause is a dataset with almost no rows on one side of the answer. Check the split first."],[1908,"The error reads \"One side of the split has only one answer, so no score can be computed\". A time split can put all the 1s on one side. Change the split to `split_method=\"random\"`, which is stratified for yes or no. See Hold-out."],[858,"The error reads \"One side of the split has only one answer, so no score can be computed\". No score can be computed when one side has only 0s or only 1s. Change the split to `split_method=\"random\"`, which is stratified for yes or no."],[629,"The error reads \"use app_rollback in Dash Server\". The published revision is not healthy. Roll back there, t