ch part shows."],[1091,"The first argument holds the roles you change. The second holds the slots. With nothing to change, the saved approval stands."],[2516,"The first cell of `00_quickstart` checks the kit and changes nothing:"],[1520,"The first cell of `00_quickstart` picks the dataset:"],[1752,"The first cell of `01_explore` holds the settings. `DATASET` picks the data. `USE_PREPARED = True` makes the model read the typed, cleaned view that Prepare creates. The lab cell looks like this:"],[3689,"The first dataset that was loaded, in the order given; says which one is used."],[4051,"The first dataset that was loaded, in the order given; says which one is used."],[2858,"The first shows the checks, the question, the answers and the data table. The second then asks the install question live."],[895,"The first writes `notebook/docs/out`; the second copies it to `notebook/docs/site` and stamps it with a digest of the sources."],[2103,"The five checks nb.data_checks runs on a table, what each finding means for a result, and what the fraud_transactions and amazon_sales checks report."],[1655,"the five kinds of idea it may suggest"],[3504,"The five roles of earlier descriptors, read from the slots: \\{role: column}, \\{text: \\[column]}."],[1958,"The fix"],[1965,"The fix in every case is the same: write it again with `nb.write_handoff(conn, ds)`. The old file stays where it is. The file name carries the descriptor version, so the new one is saved beside the old one."],[3183,"The fix is printed first, with the error."],[772,"The flagged count, the share, and what sets the flagged rows apart"],[562,"The flow"],[1210,"The flow"],[1740,"The flow"],[2186,"The flow"],[2292,"The flow"],[1484,"The flow is the same for all three origins. Only the first loading step differs. A sample dataset loads once and stays. Your file is copied into a table. An external source stays where it is: the kit creates a **virtual schema**, a schema in your local database whose tables point at a database or lake somewhere else. Its name is `VS_<NAME>`, where `<NAME>` is the name you gave the connection. Its tables are read when you query them."],[535,"The folder and zip that let a colleague repeat your work, what each file holds, and how to rerun it."],[3561,"The folder holds the approved column map (`descriptor.yaml`), the schema map when there is one\n(`schema-map.json`), every query the workflow ran with its digest and row count (`queries.sql`), the workflow log\nas it is (`workflow.jsonl`) and a `README.md` that says what the data is, which column map version the numbers rest\non, which questions were approved, which dashboards were published and how to repeat it. Nothing in it is a password:\nthe log is already written without one, and the connection is the kit's."],[3542,"The folder that holds the versions of the descriptor `id` (whether or not it exists yet); an id that is not\na dataset id is refused, so no path is built from it."],[3667,"The folder that holds the versions of the descriptor `id` (whether or not it exists yet); an id that is not a dataset id is refused, so no path is built from it."],[3192,"The four layers"],[1337,"The four months add up to 128,969, the same as `SELECT COUNT(*)` on the view."],[2959,"The full reference of an installed add-on is on this computer."],[1850,"The full signatures are in the model lab reference."],[987,"The full table, including what an update touches and the schemas in the database, is in Where things live."],[647,"The functions that check the data and the model before you rely on a score, and what each one reports."],[170,"The grant comment says `SELECT ANY DICTIONARY` is deliberately not granted, so system dictionaries stay private. `_exakit_assert_mcp_readonly_posture()` checks that the user holds those three and no other system privilege, directly, through a role, or as an object grant. It also runs a write probe that must be rejected. The install stops if any check fails."],[1082,"the groups to compare"],[1141,"The groups to compare"],[2588,"The guided card and the Code view"],[2129,"The guided card shows the findings under plain titles: **Repeated IDs**, **Repeated rows**, **Empty cells**, **Placeholder values** and **Rare outcome**."],[903,"The guided view"],[916,"The guided view"],[957,"The guided view"],[534,"The hand-off bundle"],[536,"The hand-off bundle"],[1959,"The hand-off bundle has no such check"],[1967,"The hand-off bundle, from The hand-off bundle, is a snapshot too. It has no staleness check. Write a new one when the work changes."],[3592,"The handoff as a dict: from a path to the file, from `<id>-v<n>` (that version's file), or from a descriptor\nid (the file with the highest version in the handoffs folder). None there is a FileNotFoundError saying how to\nmake one."],[3775,"The handoff as a dict: from a path to the file, from `<id>-v<n>` (that version's file), or from a descriptor id (the file with the highest version in the handoffs folder)."],[3586,"The handoff from the Data Scientist to the ML Engineer (DS-3 -> ML-3, design 4): one JSON file that holds everything\n`01_train` needs to train without opening the descriptor - which descriptor version and digest it came from, a digest\nof the data, the feature view built from the roles (as `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW` SQL), the label and its positive\nclass, the training SELECT with a reproducible time split, and the input and output schemas. A descriptor without a date\nrole has no time split: the handoff is refused with the fix (`split=\"random\"` for a reproducible hash split, or a\ndate role) - a random split is never silent, and `01_train` refuses one the file does not mark as asked for."],[3581,"The handoff from the Data Scientist to the ML Engineer (DS-3 -> ML-3, design 4): one JSON file that holds everything 01_train needs to train without opening the descriptor - which descriptor version and digest it came from, a digest of the data, the feature view built from the roles (as CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW SQL), the label and its positive class, the training SELECT with a reproducible time split, and the input and output schemas."],[3589,"The handoff record for `ds` as design 4 shapes it, built now and returned, nothing written: the descriptor's\nid, version, digest (`digest_kind` says whether it is the table digest or the file's) and source; the data\ndigest (`data_digest_kind`); the feature view; the label and its positive class (`label` overrides it);\nthe split (`time` over the date role by default; `split=\"random\"` for a hash split, the only way to get one -\nwithout a date role and without that, a ValueError names the fix; `split.explicit` records the choice) and\n`train_share`; the training SQL; input and output schemas. `workflow_id` defaults to the active workflow's."],[3695,"The handoff record for `ds` as design 4 shapes it, built now and returned, nothing written: the descriptor's id, version, digest (`digest_kind` says whether it is the table digest or the file's) and source; the data digest (`data_digest_kind`); the feature view; the label and its positive class (`label` overrides it); the split (`time` over the date role by default; `split=\"random\"` for a hash split, the only way to get one - without a date role and without that, a ValueError names the fix; `split.explicit` records the choice) and `train_share`; the training SQL; input and output schemas."],[4106,"The headless decision for EXAKIT_NB_AUTO_APPROVE=`mode`: `force` approves the proposal; `1` approves only a proposal whose roles equal the current approved version's - then nothing is written (`unchanged`); a proposal that differs stays a `draft` and the reason is printed."],[627,"The health check failed, so the kit will not publish it. The error reads \"Dash Server preview is unhealthy\". Open the Dash Server log with `exakit logs dash-server`, fix the cause, and run the preview again."],[1723,"The held-out score is above 0.98 for yes or no, or above 0.995 for a number"],[902,"The helpers"],[913,"The helpers"],[954,"The helpers"],[2007,"The helpers are in the workflow reference."],[3074,"The helpers refuse a password, a token, a secret or an API key, and a connection string with a password in it. The log never holds a credential."],[900,"The helpers, the guided cards and the kit - which part owns what, and the rules that keep them honest."],[673,"The hold-out on fraud_transactions"],[2366,"The Home card lists the models your key may use. Reasoning models (the `o` series, `gpt-5` and later) take an effort setting."],[2352,"The Home card of `00_quickstart` sets the model up from menus. You never type an address or a model name unless you want to."],[2960,"The Home card's **Marketplace** tab does the same with buttons. See Run the kit from a notebook."],[3244,"The hospital chain reference build uses this pattern on three systems that share no patient id: a legacy system on PostgreSQL, a health record on MongoDB and an imaging archive on Amazon S3. Exasol reads all three in place, resolves 1,609 patient records to 1,223 people, and answers a question across them in 0.3 to 4 seconds. The images never pass through the database."],[1608,"The host name does not resolve"],[4317,"The id is `wf-YYYYMMDD-<6 hex>`, kept in the session store and in EXAKIT_WORKFLOW_ID so a child process (a CLI call\nfrom a notebook) inherits it. The log is `$EXAKIT_HOME/workflows/<id>.jsonl` (EXAKIT_HOME default\n\\~/.exasol-starter-kit), append-only, one JSON object per line: `{ts, workflow_id, kind, ...payload}`; the folder is\n0700 and the file 0600, each line one O_APPEND write so two kernels on one workflow never interleave. A log that\ncannot be written warns and the cell goes on: logging never fails the work it describes."],[2638,"The id that every query and decision in a piece of work carries, the log file it writes, and what is never written to it."],[1724,"The idea probably gives the answer away. Do not keep it."],[859,"The idea you tried scored above 0.98 on the held-out rows, and the verdict is `suspicious`. The kit says \"The score jumped almost to perfect. That usually means the idea gives the answer away. Don't keep it.\" Do not keep it. For the whole model, run `nb.leakage_check` and read `lab_data.left_out`. See Leakage."],[2167,"The ideas"],[2171,"The ideas come from rules. They include:"],[1706,"The ideas the kit makes"],[2562,"The install command for each system, the Data Scientist option, what the install puts on this computer, and how to wait for it to finish."],[498,"The installer adds the kit's `bin` folder to your shell profile, through `ensure_path_hint()` in `setup/lib/common.sh`. If `EXAKIT_NO_PROFILE_EDIT=1` is set, it prints the line for you to add instead. The full uninstall leaves that line in place, as the comment above `exakit_uninstall_run()` in `setup/lib/common.sh` says. Remove the line yourself if you want it gone."],[258,"The installer log is created at mode 600 (`setup/lib/common.sh`, the `EXAKIT_LOG_FILE` block)."],[4112,"The ipywidgets picker, or None when this is not a Jupyter kernel with ipywidgets."],[1255,"The joined view brings linked columns into one wide view of one table. It is built in the Prepare stage: see Linked tables and the joined view."],[2853,"The keys"],[3892,"The kinds of external source `exakit connect` takes, as its own menu lists them (the kit's source engine,\n`catalog`): \\{id, name, summary, aliases, tier, status, version, gate} - `version` the adapter the kit pins,\n`gate` why one is not available yet (empty when it is)."],[3639,"The kinds of external source `exakit connect` takes, as its own menu lists them (the kit's source engine, `catalog`): \\{id, name, summary, aliases, tier, status, version, gate} - `version` the adapter the kit pins, `gate` why one is not available yet (empty when it is)."],[1429,"The kinds the kit can connect"],[910,"The kit"],[951,"The kit"],[1288,"The kit accepts one `SELECT` or `WITH` statement. It refuses a second statement, and any statement that writes, such as `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `CREATE`, `DROP`, `GRANT` or `COPY`."],[20,"The kit and its personas"],[3853,"The kit as it is now, in a few lines: installed or not, the database, each add-on service and its address, the\ndatasets loaded. Never raises: without the command it says where it looked. Returns the status document (\\{} when\nthere is none)."],[3703,"The kit as it is now, in a few lines: installed or not, the database, each add-on service and its address, the datasets loaded."],[401,"The kit calls a model only when you set one. Set `EXAKIT_MODEL_ID` and `EXAKIT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL`, and the helpers below call that address. The request is Chat Completions. Every model client is built by `create_client()` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/model_config.py`. The model is your choice, and the kit does not check the provider's data-retention terms."],[3113,"The kit carries sample datasets to practise on. `exakit data-load` lists them and loads the ones you pick."],[480,"The kit changes a table you own only when you tell it to. Three paths can do so."],[2084,"The kit checks columns and types when you describe. It does not compare every row on each run. Rows are checked when you approve, and again by a training hand-off's staleness check."],[1678,"The kit checks two things. It drops a suggestion that names the target, and a suggestion that names a column the data does not have. It reads up to four suggestions and no more."],[3003,"The kit connects AI clients to the database through its AI bridge. A client connected this way reads data as the kit's read-only user."],[1442,"The kit connects through its source engine, the same engine `exakit connect` runs."],[1031,"The kit copy: scripts, datasets, the notebook package and this documentation"],[1790,"The kit decides the inputs from the roles in the column map. You do not type column names. The table gives each role's treatment."],[617,"The kit does not choose a random split for you. `split=\"random\"` is a reproducible hash of each row's role columns, so the same rows always give the same split."],[1542,"The kit does not clean this table. The cleaning is part of the work, and it is covered in Prepare."],[660,"The kit does not compute a p-value, a confidence interval or a significance test. A p-value is a number that says how likely a gap this size would be by chance alone. A confidence interval is a range the true score probably falls in. The kit gives you the parts you need to judge a difference yourself."],[526,"The kit does not deploy a model from these notebooks."],[1108,"The kit drafts the column map from the table itself. The draft is a proposal. You approve it before any other step reads it. Approval checks the map and saves it as a new version. The steps are on Approve the column map."],[742,"The kit guards against leakage in three places."],[267,"The kit has no scheduled password rotation. When `mcp-setup` runs, it reapplies the stored read-only password with `ALTER USER`. It generates a new one only when the stored value is missing or fails validation."],[212,"The kit holds three kinds of secret: the database admin password (the SYS user), the password of the read-only user that AI clients use, and any model or Langfuse key you give the notebooks. Each secret is a file in one folder, `~/.exasol-starter-kit/credentials/`. A password is never written to `exakit.env`, and the manifest records the path of each credential file, not the password."],[146,"The kit leaves an installation it did not make alone:"],[315,"The kit listens on this computer only. Its outbound calls are few and listed below. The one that needs the most care is the database connection, because the database uses a self-signed certificate that no client can check against a certificate authority."],[1425,"The kit marks a column as sensitive when its values look like personal data: e-mail addresses, phone numbers, IBANs or postcodes. A sensitive column is never charted, grouped or sent to a model, and it is never exposed in the semantic model. The card says so beside the column. A sensitive column cannot be the target, a category or an entity."],[1584,"The kit names the reason in one line. The usual ones:"],[1168,"The kit never chooses the **target**, the outcome a model predicts. You do. It must be a column of 0 and 1, or true and false. The card reads the target's values and says how common the outcome is. The rule is in `target_verdict` in `notebook/package/exakit_guide/screens/understand.py`:"],[2651,"The kit never writes a key into a cell, the code view or the log."],[2489,"The kit never writes a key into a cell, the code view or the workflow log."],[3295,"The kit only rebuilds models it built. A model of the same name that someone else made is left alone."],[74,"The kit pins what it downloads. Each pin is a version in `versions.json`, and where the kit can check the file, a SHA-256 digest. The download is checked before anything is unpacked, and a file that fails the check is removed."],[1109,"The kit profiles the table in SQL and gives every column one of eight roles. It also records the evidence that chose the role."],[4,"The kit puts an Exasol database on this computer and sets it up for the work you do. What it sets up depends on your **persona**. Pick a section below, or in the list at the top of the sidebar."],[2092,"The kit reads the table's values, not only its names. It then proposes these changes:"],[2318,"The kit reads these names. Set only what you use. A value you leave out switches its feature off. It breaks nothing."],[316,"The kit records the database's certificate fingerprint once, then every client it builds checks that the port still serves that certificate."],[275,"The kit runs on your computer. It installs a local Exasol database, connects AI clients to it, and gives notebooks a way to ask questions. Its outbound calls are listed on the Network and TLS page."],[13,"The kit runs this workflow on an Exasol database on your computer. Four notebooks take you through the stages in order. Each notebook continues the same workflow, so you paste no id and copy nothing between them."],[443,"The kit sends what the table above lists. Your model provider handles that data under its own terms."],[1519,"The kit ships two sample datasets, so you can run every stage before you bring your own data. `amazon_sales` is a clean table of orders. `fraud_transactions` has repeats, gaps and placeholders on purpose, so the cleaning steps have something to find. Both load with the same cell."],[2180,"The kit tests each idea on rows the model did not train on. An idea helps when the score rises in cross-validation and does not fall on the held-out rows. An idea that pushes a yes-or-no score almost to perfect is marked suspicious, usually because it gives the answer away. The card does not let you keep a suspicious idea."],[2375,"The kit uses Anthropic's OpenAI-compatible layer, which has known limits. It is for text chat. The kit does not use the native Claude API."],[795,"The kit uses two cut-offs for the share of the rarer answer. They do different jobs, so the numbers differ."],[385,"The kit version, from `exakit status --json`."],[688,"The kit warns when the held-out set is too small to trust:"],[458,"The kit writes to two places: your database and your computer. This page lists what it creates, what it changes, and what it will not touch."],[2380,"The kit's connection is Ollama's OpenAI-compatible API. Keep the key unset. Ollama's cloud models have their own sign-in, which is separate from this setup."],[3895,"The kit's notebooks in order (\\{id, persona, step, title, summary, file}), from its notebook catalog; only\n`persona`'s when given. `file` is the path under the notebooks folder (starter-kit-notebooks/...)."],[3759,"The kit's notebooks in order (\\{id, persona, step, title, summary, file}), from its notebook catalog; only `persona`'s when given."],[3841,"The kit's own commands, run from a notebook cell with their output shown as it comes - so nobody has to leave the notebook for a terminal to check the kit, update it, install an add-on or load a dataset."],[3888,"The kit's parts as `exakit version --json` reports them: \\{id, installed, available, status, add_on,\nownership, remedy}, with a status of current, ahead, update_available, available (an add-on not installed) or\nmissing; `available` is the version the kit would install or update to."],[3627,"The kit's parts as `exakit version --json` reports them: \\{id, installed, available, status, add_on, ownership, remedy}, with a status of current, ahead, update_available, available (an add-on not installed) or missing; `available` is the version the kit would install or update to."],[3765,"The kit's personas (personas/\\*.json): \\{id, title, summary, datasets, addons, visible}."],[3900,"The kit's personas (personas/\\*.json): \\{id, title, summary, datasets, addons, visible}."],[2288,"The kit's queries cannot read such names without quotes. `nb.use_table` then creates a view with plain names in `EXAKIT_PREPARED`, and uses it. The message says so in one line. The table itself is unchanged. See Prepared views."],[1271,"The kit's rules. They match common question shapes: a rate, count, total or average per group, a top N, a trend over time, a filter by year or by a group's value."],[1251,"The kit's search is in `describe_schema` in `notebook/package/exakit_nb/schema_map.py`. Each link it finds shows the evidence beside it."],[3263,"The kit's semantic layer is **Exasol Semantic Views**, the database's own semantic layer. A model says what your data means in business terms: its entities (tables), the relationships between them, the dimensions you group by and the metrics you ask for. AI clients read it over MCP and answer from its metrics; notebooks ask it with Semantic SQL."],[3253,"The kit's semantic layer is Exasol Semantic Views - one model per schema from your approved column maps, or one model across any number of sources, kept current."],[2312,"The kit's settings file"],[1145,"The kit's shipped datasets have these slots:"],[1877,"The lab does not rely on one model. For each task it trains every model in a list on the same training rows, and it picks one. This page lists the models, the rule for the pick, and how to choose a model yourself. Every model is from scikit-learn. The key in brackets is the name you pass as `model=`."],[2179,"The lab leaves out the columns that give the answer away and the personal data. See Columns that give the answer away."],[1815,"The lab reads at most 50,000 rows (`ROW_LIMIT` in the code). When the table has a date, those are the newest rows. Otherwise the query sorts by its first column and keeps 50,000 rows."],[694,"The lab reads the newest 50,000 rows, since the table has 128,975. It learns from the oldest 75% of those and is tested on the newest 25%. The output line reads: \"The oldest 75% by ORDER_DATE to learn from, the newest 25% to test on. The model never sees the future.\""],[850,"The lab scores each model by cross-validated ROC AUC and picks from those scores (the rule is on The models the lab tries). It reports the held-out ROC AUC beside each one."],[808,"The lab scores with PR AUC instead of ROC AUC"],[1702,"The lab trains many models on the same rows: The model lab. Back to Model."],[1907,"The lab was asked to predict yes or no, but the column map has no yes-or-no target. The error reads \"Nothing to predict was chosen\". Pick a 0 or 1 column as the target in the column map, or choose another task: predict a number, find natural groups or find unusual rows."],[527,"The lab's fitted model is kept only in your kernel. Nothing saves or serves it. See Saving, MLflow and training in the database."],[672,"The lab's hold-out on amazon_sales"],[849,"The lab's own `baseline` model always gives the same answer, so its ROC AUC is 0.5. Any real model should score above it."],[803,"The label is reported as rare"],[446,"The Langfuse address rule is not applied outside `use_model()`."],[2335,"The Langfuse public key, which starts `pk-lf-`"],[2338,"The Langfuse secret key, which starts `sk-lf-`"],[165,"The launcher starts the Exasol MCP server as a sidecar. Its own default connects it as the admin. The kit's comment in `setup/lib/mcp.sh` says so: \"the launcher connects it as SYS ... the kit connects it as its read-only MCP user.\" `_mcp_sidecar_definition()` in the same file writes the kit's definition, with `EXA_USER` set to the read-only user and `EXA_PASSWORD` read from `mcp_readonly_password`."],[230,"The launcher's `sidecars.yaml` holds a reference to `mcp_readonly_password`, not the password. The file is written with mode 0600. `_mcp_sidecar_definition()` in `setup/lib/mcp.sh` says: \"The password is a file reference the editor resolves; it is never in this text.\""],[1254,"The links become relationships in the semantic model. See Semantic views."],[4182,"The links between tables of different schemas - sample data, your files, external sources - where no foreign\nkey says so and the names differ, found from the values, each with its evidence (`exakit describe --links`).\nBounded reads only; nothing is written. `ai` (default: when a model is configured) lets your model propose more\npairs from names, types and notes; `check` is pairs you have in mind (\"S.T.COL=S.T.KEY\"); each is checked against\nthe data and comes back with its evidence or under \"rejected\". Returns \\{links, partial, ...}; a link's\n`confidence` is \"low\" for plain numbers, which match by chance: confirm those before using them."],[3687,"The links between tables of different schemas - sample data, your files, external sources - where no foreign key says so and the names differ, found from the values, each with its evidence (`exakit describe --links`)."],[1828,"The list is a lead, not proof. A value with few rows can look strong by chance."],[1944,"The log grows. Every rerun adds lines to the same workflow."],[2646,"The log is one file per workflow: `~/.exasol-starter-kit/workflows/<id>.jsonl`. Each line is a JSON object. The file is append-only and readable by you only. Ids look like `wf-20261027-3f9a1c`."],[2000,"The log never holds a credential. The helpers refuse any field named password, token, secret or api_key, and a connection string with a password in it. SQL that looks credential-bearing, such as a statement with a PASSWORD or API_KEY column, is logged with its text replaced and its digest kept, and a warning is shown."],[1549,"The map has one table per origin: external sources first, then your data, then the samples. Each table shows its rows, its column map state and its roles. Under the tables it lists the links between them."],[1561,"The map returns a DataFrame through `data.tables`, `data.columns` and `data.links`."],[1013,"The Marimo versions"],[3884,"The marketplace's add-ons as `exakit marketplace --list --json` reports them: \\{id, name, about, status,\ninstalled, version, url}, and `offered`: False for one the kit's catalog does not offer yet (its\nmarketplace/catalog.json says \"visible\": false, so no marketplace screen lists it)."],[3603,"The marketplace's add-ons as `exakit marketplace --list --json` reports them: \\{id, name, about, status, installed, version, url}, and `offered`: False for one the kit's catalog does not offer yet (its marketplace/catalog.json says \"visible\": false, so no marketplace screen lists it)."],[251,"The master password is read from its file and passed as `SCS_MASTER_PASSWORD` to the store tool"],[223,"The master password of the notebook connection store"],[291,"The MCP server connection is not pinned here"],[310,"The MCP server connection is not pinned in this code"],[199,"The MCP server has no authentication token. Its protection is the loopback address and the read-only user."],[288,"The MCP server has no token"],[338,"The MCP server has no token. See AI clients."],[371,"The MCP server's connection to the database is not pinned in this repository."],[2100,"The measured semantic model was built on `EXAKIT_PREPARED.AMAZON__ORDER_LINES`, the typed view of `AMAZON.ORDER_LINES`. The view holds 128,969 rows and the table 128,975. Every row of the table is distinct (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM AMAZON.ORDER_LINES)` returns 128,975), so the 6 rows are not exact copies. The view's own SQL (`SELECT VIEW_TEXT FROM SYS.EXA_ALL_VIEWS WHERE VIEW_NAME = 'AMAZON__ORDER_LINES'`) says why: it trims every text value, turns an empty one into NULL, and then keeps one row of each group that is the same in every column except `ORDER_LINE_ID` (`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY` every other column `ORDER BY ORDER_LINE_ID)`, `WHERE COPY_ = 1`). Six order lines were copies of another line apart from their row id, and the view dropped them. The semantic model's measurements are on Semantic views."],[1597,"The message is `<name> was not connected:` and the lines after it give the reason. The usual causes:"],[1411,"The message is a `DescriptorNotApproved` error. Explore, Ask and Share do not read a draft. Open `00_quickstart` and approve the map. This is the same error as the entry above."],[2500,"The message names the missing setting. Tracing needs all three: the public key, the secret key and the base URL. A plain `http://` address to a host outside this computer and your network also turns tracing off. Use the `https://` address."],[2631,"The message names the missing setting. Tracing needs all three: the public key, the secret key and the base URL. A plain `http://` address to a host outside this computer and your network also turns tracing off. Use the `https://` address."],[1756,"The messy dataset, `fraud_transactions`, has a rare answer, no date and placeholder values. Its differences are listed in The model lab."],[1772,"The messy dataset: fraud_transactions"],[1770,"The metric and the split for amazon_sales"],[759,"The metric the lab picks by"],[3134,"The ML Engineer persona is not offered in this release, so for now only Data Scientist and General load these datasets."],[531,"The ML Engineer's training runs `nb.prepare_training`, which is described on the model page. It writes the coefficients of a trained logistic regression beside the training hand-off, so a scoring notebook can read them without MLflow."],[4227,"The MLflow server this kernel records runs to: `tracking_uri` (empty: the kit's own MLflow, or what the shell\nset); `login=True` asks for the server's username and password (hidden). Prints where runs go, never a password."],[3829,"The MLflow server this kernel records runs to: `tracking_uri` (empty: the kit's own MLflow, or what the shell set); `login=True` asks for the server's username and password (hidden)."],[1385,"The model also lists each table it is built on and whether it is a table in this database or an external source read live."],[3233,"The model compiled that into one scan, one join and one `GROUP BY`. A hand-written join returned the same figures for all 128,975 rows."],[3281,"The model is named after the schema (`amazon`) and its views are published in `SEM_<SCHEMA>` (`SEM_AMAZON.ORDER_LINES`). Approving a schema's links (`exakit describe --schema AMAZON --approve`) rebuilds it with the links as relationships."],[1684,"The model is never asked to approve anything, and it never changes your data."],[1680,"The model is the endpoint you set. It is called only when you pass `ai=True`, or when you run the themes step. With a model set up, feature ideas send names and roles, never rows."],[806,"The model lab"],[1765,"The model lab"],[3904,"The model lab: nb.model_lab - the Data Scientist chooses the task, the model and the split."],[2484,"The model may never do three things:"],[4018,"The model providers the notebooks know, the models a key can use, and the effort each model takes."],[2470,"The model sees only what each step needs. It never sees rows."],[2402,"The model's default"],[2323,"The model's id, exactly as your provider or server names it"],[2067,"The model's inputs or settings changed"],[2329,"The model's key. Leave it unset for a local server that asks for no key."],[1309,"The model's structure follows the column maps:"],[1871,"The models the lab tries"],[1873,"The models the lab tries"],[1465,"The name (alias) first: lower case, for example `sales`, and its tables appear as `VS_SALES`. Then every required input, such as the host, the database, the account and the password."],[2953,"The name, the link and the About text of an add-on come from its own repository on GitHub, as last looked up. When a lookup fails, the kit's own copy is shown."],[518,"The name: `amazon-sales-by-category`"],[875,"The new helper now has an entry in its module's reference page and a row in All commands. `nb.docs(nb.your_helper)` opens it once the site is rebuilt."],[680,"The newest rows. The model learns from the oldest and is tested on the newest, so it never sees the future. Cross-validation uses time-ordered folds (`TimeSeriesSplit`)."],[1059,"The next **version** is written to the kit's store on this computer, under `~/.exasol-starter-kit/descriptors/`."],[848,"The note also says that 50% is guessing, and that accuracy is not the score when the answer is rare."],[828,"The note is printed under the table and also returned as a string, so you can copy it next to the figures in a report."],[3024,"The notebook add-on brings JupyterLab and Marimo, the kit's notebooks and the helpers they use. Install it with a persona that has notebooks, or with `exakit marketplace notebook`."],[1689,"The notebook baseline"],[693,"The notebook baseline splits at random, even though `amazon_sales` has a date. The lab splits by time. The two scores are measured on different rows, so do not compare them as if they were one test."],[594,"The notebook calls `nb.dash_tools()`, which starts Dash Server if it is stopped. Dash Server is a kit add-on. The add-on is installed with `exakit marketplace dash-server`. The add-ons page explains how add-ons work."],[250,"The notebook connection store"],[266,"The notebook connection store is encrypted, but its master password is a file in the same credentials folder. The store protects the connection details from a casual reader. It does not protect them from anyone who can read that folder."],[691,"The notebook holds out a quarter of the rows, scores the model on them, and prints the note:"],[623,"The notebook installs and starts it when you call `nb.dash_tools()` or `nb.addon_ready(\"dash-server\")`. To do it yourself, run `exakit marketplace dash-server`, then `exakit start dash-server`, then `exakit status` to confirm. See Add-ons."],[521,"The notebook prints the query and its digest first, so you can read the SQL before it goes anywhere. Nothing is sent to Dash Server until you run the next cell."],[2501,"The notebook server read its environment when it started. Run `exakit notebook stop`, then `exakit notebook` from the terminal that has the variables. A kernel restart does not read them again, and a new terminal does not reach a server that is already running."],[2632,"The notebook server read its environment when it started. Run `exakit notebook stop`, then `exakit notebook` from the terminal that has the variables. A kernel restart does not read them again, and a new terminal does not reach a server that is already running."],[2451,"The notebook server reads its environment from the terminal that starts it. Its kernels read theirs from the server. So set everything in one terminal, then start the notebooks from that terminal. Every notebook then has the model and Langfuse."],[994,"The notebook sources are `notebook/notebooks/<persona>/<NN>_<name>.py`, in jupytext percent format. The `.ipynb` files and the Marimo versions are built from them:"],[1736,"The notebook's `01_explore` does this for the first three ideas. It keeps the ones with the verdict `helps` and drops the others. A kept idea is used on the next run of the lab cell."],[735,"The notebook's comment puts the problem this way: such a column \"predicts the target perfectly on old rows and fails on new rows.\""],[3649,"The notebook's Dash Server tool adapter; each call is an MCP tool call."],[3377,"The notebook's Dash Server tool adapter; each call is an MCP tool call. When Dash Server is not installed or\nnot running, it is installed and started first, the way nb.addon_ready(\"dash-server\") does it (`exakit\nmarketplace dash-server`, `exakit start dash-server`), with its progress shown: a cell that previews a dashboard\nworks on its own, and nothing is asked. `ready=False` only reports: it raises when the server is not there."],[692,"The notebook's hold-out is a stratified random split with `random_state=0`. The same rows are held out on every rerun, as long as the table has not changed."],[943,"The notebooks"],[1231,"The notebooks keep their choices in their first cell. `DATASET = None` follows the dataset you approved last."],[2590,"The notebooks run in your browser, on the kit's own Python. Both JupyterLab and Marimo open with the kernel **Exasol (Starter Kit)**. That kernel knows where the kit's database connection is, so no notebook asks for a host, a user or a password."],[3011,"The notebooks send a model names, types, notes and a column's most frequent values for most AI steps. One step sends row text: the themes step in Explore (`nb.themes`) sends the text column of sampled rows, up to 600 characters each, to sort them into themes. Every AI step is off until you set a model up. See What goes to a model and AI and tracing."],[3545,"The notebooks' documentation, opened from a cell: nb.docs()."],[824,"The notes take their words from the column map. The grain \"one row per order line\" makes the note say \"order lines\", and the count column is named `ORDER_LINES`. Three helpers do that naming:"],[1843,"The number of cross-validation folds on the training rows. The default is 5."],[769,"The number of groups tried, and the score for each"],[4217,"The optional settings, set from a notebook cell: your model and Langfuse (use_model), the MLflow server (use_mlflow)."],[1681,"The optional themes step in `01_explore` is different. It sends a sample of the newest texts on each side of the target to your model, so it needs a text column. The practice datasets have none, so the step skips."],[1897,"The order is: logistic regression, linear, ridge, logistic L1, lasso, naive Bayes, decision tree, k-nearest neighbours, SVM, random forest, extra trees, gradient boosting. The baseline is not on that list. It is picked only when it has the highest score and no model on the list is within 0.01 of it."],[66,"The order of checks"],[1079,"the outcome a model predicts"],[1139,"The outcome to predict"],[239,"The password goes in the environment variable `EXAKIT_PROFILE_PASSWORD`, read by a Python writer. It is never in argv"],[1468,"The password goes into a hidden box. It is never shown, saved or logged by the card."],[220,"The password of the read-only MCP user"],[1602,"The password or user is refused"],[2898,"The personas in this kit"],[1898,"The pick is made on cross-validation. The held-out rows are scored after the pick, for reading. They do not choose the model."],[4081,"The picker is the descriptor itself: one dropdown per column with the eight roles (key, time, measure, category, flag,\ntext, identifier, other), set to the role the rules drafted and labelled with the evidence that chose it; then one\ndropdown per slot - the columns the notebooks use (key, time, text, target, category, entity) - set to the drafted\ncolumn; then an Approve button. In JupyterLab it is\nipywidgets; in Marimo the review itself is a `mo.ui.dictionary` of `mo.ui.dropdown` with a `mo.ui.run_button`, so\n`ds = review.dataset()` runs again when the button is clicked and stops cleanly (`mo.stop`) until then; in a plain\nPython session `review.approve({\"COLUMN\": \"role\", ...})` is the same path."],[370,"The pin is recorded on first use. It trusts w